(window.TPR_CH = window.TPR_CH || []).push(
{"id":"c-t-13","n":1313,"prog":"Core: first weeks and on call","icon":"🧠","title":"Frontal lobe check after a head injury","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 11:00, neuropsychiatry clinic","where":"Neuropsychiatry outpatient clinic room","text":"Callum Okafor, a 27-year-old man, was a car passenger in a motorway crash just over a year ago and had a traumatic brain injury, then months in hospital and rehabilitation. He lives with his parents. His GP writes that the family find him more impulsive and that his jokes upset people; Callum notices little change. Your consultant wants a bedside frontal lobe picture before neuropsychology."},"task":"Carry out a focused bedside frontal lobe assessment: brief history, verbal fluency, abstraction, estimates, motor sequencing, response inhibition and primitive reflexes, then summarise the findings.","first60":["Read the discharge summaries: CT or MRI findings (frontal contusions?), length of coma and post-traumatic amnesia, epilepsy, current medicines.","Set up: a table, two chairs, plain paper and a pen, a stopwatch, a tongue depressor for the pout reflex, alcohol gel.","Check glasses and hearing aids, and whether his mother or father is waiting outside for collateral later.","Plan the order: brief history, fluency, abstraction, estimates, sequencing, inhibition, reflexes. Keep it moving.","Expect jokes and over-familiarity: note them as findings, stay friendly, and keep him on task."],"ask":["'Have you noticed any change in your sense of smell or taste since the crash?'","'Have family or friends said you've changed? In what way?'","'Tell me as many F words as you can in one minute. No names of people or places.'","'What does it mean if someone says, \"Don't count your chickens before they hatch\"?'","'In what way are a table and a chair alike?'","'How tall is an average woman in this country?'","'Watch my hand: fist, edge, palm. Now you do it.'","'When I tap once, you tap twice. When I tap twice, you tap once.'"],"recognise":["Anosmia after head injury points to damage to the orbitofrontal area or shearing of the olfactory nerves.","Low letter fluency (under about ten F words a minute in a young adult) suggests frontal dysfunction; compare with category fluency (animals).","Concrete answers to proverbs and similarities ('they both have legs') show impaired abstraction.","Bizarre cognitive estimates without self-correction show poor judgement and checking.","Perseveration on Luria's sequence or an alternating pattern shows impaired motor programming and set-shifting.","Tapping on no-go trials shows poor inhibition; a grasp reflex is a frontal release sign.","Fatuous jokes, disinhibition and poor insight together form a dysexecutive picture typical of orbitofrontal injury."],"decide":[{"c":"Complete the bedside battery, record each finding, add a scored FAB and ACE-III, take collateral from his parents, and refer for formal neuropsychology and smell testing.","r":"Right: bedside tests show a pattern; scored tools and formal testing measure it, and his family describe the real-life effect.","best":true},{"c":"Stop after a normal-looking MMSE score and reassure the family.","r":"Wrong: the MMSE largely misses frontal and executive problems. A normal score does not rule out a dysexecutive syndrome.","best":false},{"c":"Diagnose a personality disorder because of his inappropriate jokes and impulsivity.","r":"Wrong: the change followed a brain injury in a previously well man. This is an acquired organic personality change, not a personality disorder.","best":false}],"law":["Consent: explain the tasks and that some are odd; touching the hand and lips needs specific consent.","Driving: a serious head injury must be reported to the DVLA; if he plans to drive, advise him to notify them and record the advice (GMC guidance on fitness to drive).","Capacity: impulsive spending raises questions about financial decisions; capacity is decision-specific and assessed separately if needed (MCA 2005).","Collateral from parents needs his consent unless it is needed to keep him safe."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Some of these tasks will seem a bit strange, and some are meant to be hard. Everyone finds some of them tricky, so just have a go."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Thanks, Callum. A few of those were harder for you, especially switching between tasks and explaining sayings. That fits with the front of the brain being affected by the crash, and more detailed tests will help us plan support."},{"to":"Consultant","s":"Anosmia, low F fluency with rule breaks, concrete proverbs and similarities, bizarre estimate, perseveration on Luria and alternating pattern, fails go/no-go, grasp reflex present. Dysexecutive picture; FAB and ACE-III next, neuropsychology referral."}],"write":"Neuropsychiatry clinic. 27M. TBI as car passenger, motorway crash ~13 months ago; months in hospital and rehab. Lives with parents. Referral: family report impulsivity and hurtful jokes; patient notices little change.\nConsent obtained; hand hygiene; no glasses or hearing aids.\nHistory: can hardly smell since the crash. Says friends call him 'a laugh'; mother says he spent £600 on online games in a weekend. Jocular, over-familiar.\nVerbal fluency: F 7 words in 60 s, including 2 proper nouns and 1 repeat; animals 12.\nAbstraction: proverb ('chickens') interpreted literally. Similarities: table/chair 'both have legs'.\nCognitive estimate: average woman's height 'about eight feet'; no self-correction.\nSequencing: Luria fist-edge-palm: could not learn after 5 demonstrations; perseverated on fist. Alternating pattern: perseverated.\nInhibition: conflicting instructions: 3 errors; go/no-go: tapped on most no-go trials.\nPrimitive reflexes: grasp present bilaterally; palmomental and pout absent; no utilisation behaviour.\nImpression: dysexecutive syndrome with disinhibition, concrete thinking, perseveration and impaired inhibition; anosmia; consistent with frontal (orbitofrontal) injury.\nPlan: FAB and ACE-III next visit; collateral from parents; formal neuropsychology referral; smell testing; mood screen; DVLA advice (not driving at present); Headway information.\nDr Chinonso, CT2 psychiatry","make":["Explain at the start that the tasks are odd and some are meant to be hard; it lowers anxiety and keeps him going.","Demonstrate the motor tasks yourself, silently, before asking him to copy.","Time fluency with a watch and write down every word, so rule breaks and repeats are visible.","Encourage a guess when he stalls; a guess is the finding.","Ask about smell: it is easily forgotten and points to the orbitofrontal area.","Summarise findings as a pattern, not a list of fails."],"next":"Callum scores 9/18 on the FAB at the next visit. Formal neuropsychology confirms a dysexecutive syndrome. His parents join a Headway carers' group, and the brain injury team sets up a budgeting plan with him and a referral to a vocational rehabilitation service.","marks":["Performed hand hygiene, confirmed identity, gained consent and explained the tasks.","Asked about smell and about changes others have noticed.","Tested letter and category fluency with timing and rules.","Tested proverbs and similarities.","Asked a cognitive estimate and encouraged a guess.","Demonstrated and tested Luria's sequence and an alternating pattern.","Tested conflicting instructions and go/no-go.","Tested grasp, palmomental and pout reflexes with consent.","Thanked him and summarised the findings."],"pitfalls":["Relying on the MMSE or a memory test alone.","Explaining Luria's test in words instead of demonstrating silently.","Forgetting to ask about the sense of smell.","Laughing along with jokes and losing the structure.","Touching his hand or lips without explaining first."],"refs":["1. Dubois B et al. The FAB: a Frontal Assessment Battery at bedside. Neurology 2000 (checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG232 Head injury: assessment and early management (2023; checked Nov 2026)","3. DVLA: Assessing fitness to drive, a guide for medical professionals (checked Nov 2026)","4. Headway, the brain injury association: information for families (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK clinics often use the FAB (scored out of 18) and the ACE-III alongside bedside frontal tests. Cognitive estimates should be culturally fair; use local examples such as the height of a double-decker bus or an average woman.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-14","n":1314,"prog":"Core: first weeks and on call","icon":"🦵","title":"Leg weakness with normal scans: lower limb neurological check","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 15:30, liaison psychiatry","where":"Medical ward, side bay","text":"Gillian Mbatha, a 43-year-old woman, suddenly lost strength in her left leg a week ago. Stroke was suspected, but scans and tests show no structural cause, and neurology have asked liaison psychiatry to see her, mentioning a painful, hostile divorce. Her named nurse tells you the leg feels even weaker since this morning."},"task":"Carry out a full neurological assessment of both lower limbs, explaining each step, including signs that support functional weakness, and summarise your findings for the neurology team.","first60":["Read the neurology notes: MRI brain and spine results, nerve studies, which signs they have already documented.","Ask the nurse what 'weaker' means today: can she still stand? Any new numbness, back pain, bladder or bowel change, or other limb involved? New signs go straight back to neurology.","Get kit: tendon hammer, 128 Hz tuning fork, cotton wool, neurotips, a chaperone, the curtain and a blanket.","Plan to examine as a neurologist would, both legs, right first; your role is to look, not to reassure early or to judge.","Keep the divorce out of the first minutes; she is here for her leg."],"ask":["'When did the weakness start, and what were you doing?'","'Any injury, back pain, or pins and needles?'","'Any change with your bladder or bowels, or numbness around your bottom?'","'Is anything painful right now?'","'Can you lift your leg off the bed for me?'","'Push your heel down into my hand. Now lift the other leg.'","'Can you feel this? Is it sharp or soft?'","'Are you able to stand and walk a few steps if I stay right next to you?'"],"recognise":["Normal tone, reflexes and plantars with marked weakness do not fit an upper or lower motor neurone pattern.","Hoover's sign: weak voluntary hip extension on the left, but normal involuntary hip extension when she flexes the right hip against resistance. A positive, specific sign of functional weakness.","Hip abductor sign: weak left abduction alone, but strong when both legs abduct together.","Give-way weakness (sudden collapse after brief normal power) and a leg dragged behind with the hip turned out are typical functional patterns.","Functional weakness is diagnosed on positive signs, not because tests are normal or because of the divorce.","Worsening needs a fresh look for new signs; functional and structural problems can coexist."],"decide":[{"c":"Complete the full neurological check, document Hoover's and the hip abductor signs, feed back to neurology, and offer a gentle, positive explanation consistent with theirs.","r":"Right: positive signs support the diagnosis, a consistent explanation builds trust, and neurology lead on the diagnosis.","best":true},{"c":"Tell her the tests are normal so it must be stress from the divorce.","r":"Harmful: it implies she is imagining it, and functional diagnosis rests on positive signs, not stress. She is likely to disengage.","best":false},{"c":"Skip the neurological check because neurology have already done one.","r":"Wrong: the leg is worse today; you must look for new signs, and your findings inform the liaison formulation.","best":false}],"law":["Consent for each part of the assessment; offer and record a chaperone (GMC guidance on intimate examinations and chaperones).","Preserve dignity: expose only what you need, with a blanket over the rest.","Share findings with the neurology team as part of her direct care; ask before discussing personal life with family.","If she has capacity, she can decline any part of the assessment; record what was done and why."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I'm sorry the leg feels worse today; that must be frightening. I'll check your legs carefully, and explain each step as I go."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Your nerves and reflexes are working normally. When you lifted your right leg, your left leg pushed down strongly by itself. That tells us the connection is there; the problem is in sending the signal on purpose, and that can improve."},{"to":"Neurology registrar","s":"Lower limbs: normal tone, reflexes and sensation, flexor plantars. Left leg weak with give-way; positive Hoover's and hip abductor signs; dragging gait. No new signs today. Supports functional weakness. Liaison will follow up."}],"write":"Liaison psychiatry. 43F. Sudden L leg weakness 7 days ago; stroke suspected; MRI brain and spine and other tests normal (neurology). Recent acrimonious divorce. Nurse reports leg weaker this morning.\nConsent; chaperone (Staff Nurse Patel); hand hygiene; exposed with dignity; no pain.\nHistory: onset at home making tea; no trauma, back pain, paraesthesia, saddle numbness, bladder or bowel change. No new symptoms today apart from feeling weaker.\nInspection: no wasting, fasciculation, scars or tremor; no walking aid.\nTone: normal both legs; no clonus.\nPower: R 5/5 throughout. L: hip flexion 1/5, extension 2/5, knee 2/5, ankle 2/5, with give-way. Hoover's sign positive (strong involuntary L hip extension on R hip flexion). Hip abductor sign positive.\nReflexes: knee and ankle jerks 2+ and symmetrical; plantars flexor bilaterally.\nCoordination: heel-shin normal on R; L limited by weakness, no ataxia.\nSensation: light touch, pinprick, vibration and joint position normal and symmetrical.\nGait: stands; drags L leg behind, hip externally rotated.\nImpression: positive signs of functional L leg weakness; no new structural signs.\nPlan: findings to neurology; would also examine upper limbs and cranial nerves and check peripheral pulses; explanation consistent with neurology; FND physiotherapy; liaison follow-up for mood and stressors.\nDr Chinonso, CT2 psychiatry","make":["Ask about pain first and examine any painful area last.","Talk to her throughout and explain what each test checks.","Compare left with right at every level.","Do Hoover's sign properly: hand under the weak heel while she lifts the good leg against your hand.","Describe positive findings to her as good news about the wiring, never as 'nothing wrong'.","Check for red flags first: new numbness, back pain, bladder or bowel change."],"next":"Neurology confirm functional limb weakness and show Gillian her Hoover's sign. She starts specialist FND physiotherapy on the ward and walks with a frame within a week. Liaison psychiatry see her twice more to explore low mood and the stress of the divorce, and refer her for outpatient psychological therapy.","marks":["Hand hygiene, identity, consent, chaperone, dignity, pain check and positioning.","Brief focused history including red flags.","Inspection for wasting, fasciculation, scars and aids.","Tone at hip, knee and ankle, with clonus.","Power at hip, knee and ankle by movement, with Hoover's sign.","Knee and ankle reflexes and plantars.","Heel-shin coordination.","Light touch, pinprick, vibration and joint position.","Gait if safe; offered to check arms, cranial nerves and pulses; summary to neurology."],"pitfalls":["Saying 'there is nothing wrong' or 'it's all stress'.","Missing Hoover's sign or doing it with the wrong leg lifted.","Forgetting the chaperone or pain check.","Ignoring the reported worsening and not looking for new signs.","Walking her without standing right beside her."],"refs":["1. NHS website: Functional neurological disorder (FND) (checked Nov 2026)","2. neurosymptoms.org: patient information on functional neurological symptoms (checked Nov 2026)","3. Royal College of Psychiatrists and Association of British Neurologists: guidance on functional neurological disorder (checked Nov 2026)","4. GMC: Intimate examinations and chaperones (2024; checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK neurologists now use 'functional neurological disorder', not 'hysterical' or 'conversion' in front of patients. The diagnosis rests on positive signs, and patients are often shown their own Hoover's sign to help them understand it.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-15","n":1315,"prog":"Old Age Psychiatry","icon":"🧩","title":"Explaining Alzheimer's disease and its management to a daughter","src":"","moment":{"when":"Friday 14:30, older adult community team","where":"Memory clinic, quiet interview room","text":"Margaret Oduya, a 78-year-old woman, was seen in clinic recently: ACE-III 46/100, HADS anxiety 4 and depression 1, MRI with marked hippocampal and parietal atrophy, normal bloods. She lives alone, manages her own daily tasks and takes alendronic acid, calcium with colecalciferol, paracetamol and lactulose. She has agreed that you can talk with her daughter, Claire, who arrives anxious."},"task":"Explain the results and the diagnosis of Alzheimer's disease in plain words, then the medication, non-drug help, safety at home and planning ahead, checking understanding throughout.","first60":["Confirm Margaret's consent to share with Claire is in the notes, and what she agreed could be shared.","Know the results cold: ACE-III 46/100, HADS low, MRI hippocampal and parietal atrophy, normal bloods including B12, folate, thyroid and syphilis.","Check the drug chart: no anticholinergics; note osteoporosis (falls matter) and resting pulse if recorded.","Have leaflets ready: Alzheimer's Society, donepezil information, LPA, carers' assessment.","Plan small chunks: results, diagnosis, treatment, home safety, planning. Pause after each."],"ask":["'How has your mum been, and what do you know so far?'","'What worries you most?'","'Have you noticed problems with cooking, bills, medicines or getting lost?'","'Does she still drive?'","'How much are you able to help, alongside your own life?'","'Has she made a lasting power of attorney?'","'Would it help if I paused there? What's going through your mind?'","'Just to check I've been clear, what will you tell your mum?'"],"recognise":["Dementia is an umbrella term for a progressive decline in memory and thinking, in clear consciousness, that affects daily life; Alzheimer's disease is the commonest type.","ACE-III 46/100 is well below the usual cut-off and, with daily-life effects, fits a moderate stage.","Hippocampal (memory) and parietal (spatial) atrophy on MRI supports Alzheimer's disease; normal bloods exclude common reversible causes.","Low HADS scores make depression an unlikely explanation.","Living alone with moderate dementia brings risks: cooking, medicines, falls (with osteoporosis), scams, getting lost and driving.","An anxious relative overwhelmed by jargon needs short, plain chunks and a pause; this is part of the task, not a distraction."],"decide":[{"c":"Explain the diagnosis plainly, offer donepezil after a pulse check, arrange cognitive stimulation therapy, an OT home visit, social care and carers' assessments, discuss LPA and driving, and give written information and a follow-up date.","r":"Right: it covers the illness, the treatment and the practical risks of living alone, and plans ahead while Margaret can still make decisions.","best":true},{"c":"Explain that nothing can be done because Alzheimer's cannot be cured.","r":"Wrong: medicines can help symptoms for a while, and non-drug support, safety measures and planning make a large difference.","best":false},{"c":"Arrange a care home placement now because she lives alone.","r":"Premature: she is independent in daily tasks, wants to stay home, and her choice must be respected; support at home comes first.","best":false}],"law":["Confidentiality: Margaret consented to sharing; keep to what she agreed and involve her in decisions.","Capacity is decision-specific (MCA 2005). While she has capacity she can make lasting powers of attorney for health and welfare and for property and financial affairs.","Driving: she must tell the DVLA about a dementia diagnosis. If she keeps driving against advice, a doctor may need to inform the DVLA (GMC guidance), after telling her.","Carers: under the Care Act 2014 the local authority must offer Claire a carer's assessment."],"say":[{"to":"Daughter","s":"Your mum's memory test was well below what we'd expect, and her scan shows shrinkage in the parts of the brain for memory and finding her way around. Put together, this is Alzheimer's disease."},{"to":"Daughter","s":"There's a tablet, donepezil, that boosts a brain chemical used for memory. It doesn't cure the illness, but it can help her memory and day-to-day function for a while."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Diagnosis: Alzheimer's disease, moderate (ACE-III 46/100). Starting donepezil 5 mg at night after pulse check; increase to 10 mg after 4 weeks if tolerated. Referred for CST and OT home safety visit. LPA and DVLA discussed."}],"write":"Memory clinic. Discussion with daughter, Claire Oduya, with Margaret's documented consent.\nPatient: Margaret Oduya, 78F. ACE-III 46/100; HADS A4 D1; MRI: global atrophy, bilateral mesial temporal and hippocampal atrophy, parietal involvement; bloods normal incl. B12, folate, TFT, syphilis. PMH osteoporosis, diverticular disease. Meds: alendronic acid 70 mg weekly, calcium/colecalciferol, paracetamol 1 g QDS, lactulose. Lives alone; independent in ADLs; no carers; no alcohol.\nClaire: anxious; knew 'the scan was back'. Concerns: burnt pan last month, missed bills, a cold caller who took her bank details, still drives to the shops, insists she is fine.\nExplained: dementia as umbrella; Alzheimer's disease, moderate stage; plaques and tangles; progressive; not her fault; depression unlikely. Claire asked me to pause when overwhelmed; re-explained in short chunks; teach-back accurate.\nPlan: donepezil 5 mg at night after pulse/ECG check, 10 mg after 4 weeks; side effects explained; memantine later if needed. CST referral. Dementia adviser. OT home safety visit, telecare. Adult social care assessment; carer's assessment for Claire. LPA (both types) while capacity present. Advised Margaret to stop driving and notify DVLA; will discuss with her. Bank fraud protection. Blister pack via pharmacy. Alzheimer's Society leaflets. Follow-up in 4 weeks with Margaret.\nDr Chinonso, CT2 psychiatry","make":["Start with what she knows and fears, not with the results.","Give results in plain words, a little at a time, and pause.","Name the diagnosis clearly; don't hide behind 'memory problems'.","Explain how the medicine works in one sentence, with the main side effects.","Make the home safety plan concrete: cooking, medicines, falls, scams, driving.","Check understanding with teach-back and give written information."],"next":"Margaret starts donepezil and tolerates 10 mg. An OT fits a cooker cut-off and a falls sensor; she joins a cognitive stimulation group. Claire and Margaret make both lasting powers of attorney with a solicitor. Margaret agrees to stop driving and returns her licence, with Claire's help.","marks":["Checked consent, what Claire knew and her concerns.","Explained the results in plain words.","Named Alzheimer's disease, explained dementia and progression, and that depression was unlikely.","Responded to her distress and paused when she was overwhelmed.","Explained donepezil: how it works, benefits, side effects, pulse check; memantine later.","Covered non-drug help and home safety, including driving and scams.","Discussed LPA, care package and carer's assessment, then checked understanding and closed."],"pitfalls":["Using jargon ('cholinesterase', 'hippocampal atrophy') without plain words.","Avoiding the word Alzheimer's.","Promising the tablet will stop the illness.","Forgetting driving, scams and falls with osteoporosis.","No written information or follow-up date."],"refs":["1. NICE NG97, dementia (2018; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE TA217 Donepezil, galantamine, rivastigmine and memantine for Alzheimer's disease (checked Nov 2026)","3. BNF: donepezil and memantine (checked Nov 2026)","4. Alzheimer's Society: information for carers (checked Nov 2026)","5. DVLA: Dementia and driving (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, the diagnosis is shared openly with the person and, with consent, their family. Lasting powers of attorney are made while the person still has capacity, so raise them early.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-16","n":1316,"prog":"Old Age Psychiatry","icon":"🧠","title":"Husband's violence: what's changed?","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 10:20","where":"Emergency department, liaison psychiatry","text":"Angela Thornbury, a 63-year-old woman, rang 999 this morning after her 65-year-old husband, Clive, grabbed her by the throat at home. Police brought her in; the emergency team have checked her neck and given pain relief. Clive is in police custody. She keeps saying, 'He's never hurt me before. He's just not Clive any more.'"},"task":"Take a history from Angela about the change in Clive's behaviour, screen for other causes, and carry out a risk assessment covering her safety tonight and the wider risks Clive now poses.","first60":["Read the ED notes: neck findings, any loss of consciousness, voice change or swallowing problems, and whether a senior has reviewed the strangulation.","Ask the police officer where Clive is now, whether he has been arrested, and whether the custody Liaison and Diversion team knows about him.","Find a private room with tissues and water; make sure Clive cannot walk in and that no relative is listening without her agreement.","Expect tears first. Plan to give her time, then move to short, focused questions once she settles.","Hold the structure: this morning, the change over time, frontal features, other causes, her risk, wider risk, then plan."],"ask":["'Can you tell me what happened this morning, step by step?'","'When he had his hands on your neck, did you black out, wet yourself or struggle to breathe?'","'When did you first feel he was not himself? Was it sudden or gradual?'","'Has he said or done things in public that embarrassed you, or that he would never have done before?'","'Has his eating changed? Any cravings, especially for sweet things?'","'How is his memory for recent events? Does he get lost or lose words?'","'Has anyone in his family had dementia or a change in personality at a young age?'","'Are you frightened of him? Has he ever threatened to kill you?'","'Is he still driving? Who looks after the money?'"],"recognise":["Two years of gradual change in personality and conduct with memory largely spared points to behavioural-variant frontotemporal dementia, not Alzheimer's disease.","The core features: disinhibition, apathy, loss of empathy, repetitive or compulsive acts, overeating or sweet cravings, and poor planning.","Lack of insight is typical: he does not see a problem, which is why the history must come from Angela.","Onset in the sixties and a parent who changed in middle age suggest a familial form; ask directly.","Sudden change, fluctuating confusion, focal weakness or a new headache would point instead to delirium, stroke or a space-occupying lesion.","Hands round the neck is a high-risk marker for future serious harm, whatever the cause."],"decide":[{"c":"Finish the history and risk assessment, explain a likely brain illness in plain words, agree she stays with her daughter tonight, complete DASH and refer to the IDVA and MARAC if high risk, tell custody that Clive needs a mental health assessment, and ask the ED team to keep her under review for delayed neck injury.","r":"Right: it protects Angela today, treats the strangulation as the serious injury it is, and starts the route to a diagnosis for Clive.","best":true},{"c":"Reassure her that this is probably dementia, so she need not worry about further violence, and let her go home.","r":"Wrong: an illness explains the change but does not lower the risk. Strangulation predicts later serious harm, and he has no insight.","best":false},{"c":"Advise her not to tell the police anything more, as Clive is unwell and should not be prosecuted.","r":"Wrong: whether he is charged is for the police and prosecutors, not you. Custody staff need the health information so he is assessed and diverted if appropriate.","best":false}],"law":["Non-fatal strangulation has been a specific criminal offence in England and Wales since June 2022. Record her account and injuries accurately.","Safeguarding: complete a DASH risk checklist; strangulation is a high-risk marker. Refer to MARAC if high risk, with her knowledge; consent is not required where risk is high.","Confidentiality: you may share relevant information with police and custody health staff to prevent serious harm; explain to Angela what you will share and why.","Clive's capacity to drive and to manage money is a later question; for now, advise that he must not drive and that dementia must be reported to the DVLA once diagnosed."],"say":[{"to":"Wife","s":"You've had a frightening morning, and your neck still hurts. There's no rush. When you're ready, I want to understand what's been going on to Clive."},{"to":"Wife","s":"What you're describing, a slow change in his manners, his eating and his feelings for others, can be caused by an illness affecting the front part of the brain. That needs proper tests. But it doesn't make today any less dangerous for you."},{"to":"Custody Liaison and Diversion team","s":"Clive Thornbury, 65, in custody after strangling his wife. Two-year history of disinhibition, loss of empathy, sweet cravings and reckless spending, memory spared, father had early dementia. Possible frontotemporal dementia. Please arrange a mental health assessment before any decision on release, and note he must not drive."}],"write":"Liaison psychiatry. Collateral from Angela Thornbury (63F), wife of Clive (65M). Seen alone, with consent.\nToday: Clive grabbed her throat with both hands when she would not give him the car keys. No LOC, no incontinence, voice hoarse, swallowing sore. Dog barking; he let go. She rang 999. Clive in police custody.\nChange over ~2 years, gradual: crude sexual remarks to strangers, comment to young woman at bakery; lost interest in grandchildren; no reaction when she cries; eats whole packets of biscuits; ~£4,000 online spending; checks locks repeatedly; stopped showering daily; denies any problem.\nNot found: memory loss, getting lost, word-finding problems, hallucinations, delusions, low mood, head injury, stroke signs, new medication. Alcohol 2-3 pints/week, unchanged. Amlodipine for BP.\nFH: father 'Pick's disease', died in a care home aged ~60.\nForensic: none. No previous violence. No weapons or firearms. No self-harm or suicidal talk from Clive.\nRisk: DASH completed, 15 ticks including strangulation; high risk. Angela frightened, no thoughts of self-harm. Grandchildren (6, 9) visit weekends. Clive still driving: two near misses.\nImpression: probable behavioural-variant FTD; high risk of further harm to Angela.\nPlan: ED senior review of neck before discharge; Angela to stay with daughter Sophie tonight; MARAC and IDVA referral; police and custody L&D informed; Clive not to drive; adult safeguarding referral; GP letter; Clive to have urgent psychiatric assessment, bloods and MRI, with memory/neurology referral.","make":["Settle her before you ask anything: notice her neck, offer time and water, and say why you're asking.","Get the morning in detail. Strangulation questions (black-out, incontinence, voice, swallowing) are medical, not voyeuristic.","Ask for examples of each change. 'He's rude' becomes evidence when you hear what he said and to whom.","Keep the differential open: memory, language, mood, psychosis, alcohol, head injury, stroke. Then ask about family history.","Separate the illness from the risk: a likely diagnosis never makes it safe for her to go home.","Close with a concrete plan for tonight, named services, and what you will tell the police."],"next":"Angela spends three weeks with her daughter. The IDVA attends the MARAC meeting with her. Clive is assessed in custody and released on bail to his brother's address. An MRI shows marked frontal and anterior temporal atrophy, and the cognitive clinic diagnoses behavioural-variant frontotemporal dementia. He stops driving, and Angela begins a carers' group run by Rare Dementia Support.","marks":["Settled her, acknowledged her injury and fear, and explained the purpose of the conversation.","Took a clear account of the assault, including strangulation severity and Clive's current whereabouts.","Elicited a gradual two-year change with specific examples of frontal features.","Screened for memory, language, mood, psychosis, substances, head injury and stroke, and asked about family history.","Assessed risk to Angela with DASH-type questions, plus risk to grandchildren, the public, driving and finances.","Explained a possible brain illness without jargon and agreed a safety plan for tonight."],"pitfalls":["Rushing into questions while she is still crying and clutching her neck.","Treating the strangulation as minor because she can talk.","Assuming dementia means memory loss and missing frontal features.","Forgetting family history in a man of 65.","Letting a likely diagnosis replace a risk assessment, and sending her home alone."],"refs":["1. NICE NG97 Dementia: assessment, management and support (2018; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG50 Domestic violence and abuse: multi-agency working (2014; checked Nov 2026)","3. SafeLives DASH risk checklist and MARAC guidance (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: Frontotemporal dementia (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, domestic abuse in older couples is a safeguarding matter, even when illness drives it. Doctors are expected to complete DASH, refer to MARAC when risk is high, and share information with police to prevent serious harm.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-17","n":1317,"prog":"Old Age Psychiatry","icon":"🌙","title":"Found wandering at night: granddaughter's account","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 21:40","where":"Acute medical unit, on call","text":"Harold Brennan, a 79-year-old retired mechanic who lives alone, was brought in by police after being found walking round his estate in pyjamas this evening. No doctor has seen him yet. His granddaughter Leah, 24, has arrived because her mother is at work. She looks pale and keeps checking her phone."},"task":"Take a collateral history from Leah about tonight and the months before, screen for delirium and physical causes, and assess the risks to Harold so that a safe decision can be made about tonight.","first60":["Ask the nurse for his observations, capillary glucose, temperature and how alert and oriented he is now; note any injuries or cold feet.","Check the police handover: where and when he was found, what he was wearing, and whether his door was open.","Check the GP summary or medication list on the shared record for his regular tablets and any recent changes.","Confirm Harold is happy for you to speak to Leah, and find a quiet room nearby.","Plan the order: tonight, the months before, delirium and physical causes, function, risk, then her own worries."],"ask":["'Can you tell me what you know about tonight: before, during and after?'","'Has anything like this happened before?'","'Over the last few months, what changes have you noticed in him?'","'Did it come on gradually, or did he suddenly get worse?'","'Has he been unwell lately: a cough, water infection, fever, or a fall?'","'What tablets does he take, and has anything changed?'","'How does he manage meals, bills, shopping and getting about?'","'How has he been since your grandmother died?'","'Does he still drive? Has anyone taken advantage of him?'"],"recognise":["A first episode of night wandering in a man living alone needs delirium excluded first: infection, retention, constipation, dehydration, drugs, pain or a head injury.","Months of slipping self-care, a gas hob left burning and denial of it suggest an underlying cognitive decline that tonight has exposed.","A spouse often covers for a partner's decline; after a death, the gaps suddenly show.","Grief and depression can look like dementia in older people; ask about mood, sleep, appetite and hopelessness.","Stepwise worsening with vascular risk factors points to vascular dementia; slow steady decline to Alzheimer's disease."],"decide":[{"c":"Complete the collateral and risk assessment, screen and test for delirium tonight, keep him in hospital until he is safe to go home, and start the route to memory assessment and a care needs assessment.","r":"Right: it treats possible delirium first, does not discharge a man at clear risk, and plans for the longer term.","best":true},{"c":"Diagnose dementia from Leah's account and discharge him home with a memory clinic referral.","r":"Wrong: delirium has not been excluded and he has nowhere safe to go tonight. A diagnosis of dementia also cannot be made in one evening.","best":false},{"c":"Start low-dose haloperidol so he won't wander on the ward.","r":"Wrong: there is no distress or danger requiring medication, and antipsychotics worsen falls and confusion. Find the cause and use nursing approaches first.","best":false}],"law":["Harold agreed to you speaking with Leah; share only what is needed and respect anything he asks you to keep private.","If he wants to leave tonight and seems confused, assess his capacity for that decision; if he lacks it, act in his best interests using the MCA, using the least restrictive option.","Safeguarding: self-neglect and risk of exploitation can be raised with the local authority under the Care Act 2014.","Driving: if dementia is later diagnosed, he must tell the DVLA; advise against driving while he is being assessed."],"say":[{"to":"Granddaughter","s":"He's safe, warm and being looked after. I'd like to ask quite a few quick questions so we can work out what's happened. Short answers are fine."},{"to":"Granddaughter","s":"I can't tell you tonight whether it's dementia. First we need to rule out an infection or something else that can make older people suddenly muddled. Either way, he won't go home alone tonight."},{"to":"Medical registrar","s":"Harold Brennan, 79, found wandering in pyjamas. Collateral: months of self-neglect, gas hob left on, gradual memory decline, wife died two months ago. No infection or falls known. Please complete delirium screen and bloods; I'll follow up for cognition and care planning."}],"write":"On-call psychiatry. Collateral from Leah Brennan (24F), granddaughter, with Harold's agreement.\nTonight: police found Harold (79M) on the estate ~20:45 in pyjamas and slippers. Front door open (father checked). Time missing unknown. No known injury. First known episode.\nPast few months: unshaven, stained clothes, fewer meals (fridge near empty), repeats questions, forgot Leah's birthday; long-term memory good. Last week gas hob burning, he insisted it was off. Gradual, worse since wife Maureen died of kidney cancer 2 months ago; she managed money and meals.\nNo recent illness, urinary symptoms, falls, head injury or hospital stays known. Meds: amlodipine 5 mg, atorvastatin 20 mg; no changes; Maureen used to give them. Alcohol: special occasions only. No smoking. No previous mental illness. Personality unchanged.\nMood: quiet, tearful at times, misses Maureen, sleeps poorly; no suicidal talk known.\nFunction: microwave meals; unpaid energy bill; still drives to the shop (new dent); a man offered roof repairs for cash last month.\nRisk: wandering, cold, gas/fire, falls, medication errors, self-neglect, exploitation, driving.\nImpression: possible delirium on a background of probable dementia; grief.\nPlan: 4AT, bloods (FBC, U&Es, CRP, glucose, LFTs, calcium, TFTs, B12, folate), urine dip, ECG; not for discharge tonight; social work referral; memory assessment once delirium excluded; advise no driving; gas safety.","make":["Earn speed: tell her you'll ask many short questions and why. Then keep them short.","Tonight before, during and after: door, clothing, how long, where found, injuries.","Ask 'gradual or sudden?' every time. The answer steers you between dementia and delirium.","Cover the physical screen properly: illness, tablets, falls, head injury, alcohol.","Risk in three parts: physical, social and psychiatric, including gas, money, driving and grief.","Contain her worry: he is safe, there is a plan, and he will not go home alone tonight."],"next":"His 4AT is positive, urine dip and CRP are normal, but he has not opened his bowels for six days. After laxatives and fluids he is clearer within three days, though his memory remains poor. The ward social worker arranges a reablement package, a gas valve and a door sensor. He is seen in the memory clinic six weeks later and diagnosed with Alzheimer's disease; he stops driving.","marks":["Reassured Leah that he is safe and set up a quick question-and-answer pace.","Took a full account of tonight: door, clothing, time, where found and injuries.","Asked whether it had happened before and established a gradual decline with examples.","Screened for delirium and physical causes, medicines, falls, head injury and alcohol.","Covered function, finances, driving, mood and grief.","Assessed physical, social and psychiatric risks, and contained Leah's distress with a clear plan."],"pitfalls":["Calling it dementia before delirium is excluded.","Skipping the night itself and jumping to memory questions.","Forgetting the gas, the car and the money.","Missing grief as a cause or contributor.","Ignoring Leah's own distress or leaving her without a plan."],"refs":["1. NICE CG103 Delirium: prevention, diagnosis and management (2010, updated 2023; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG97 Dementia: assessment, management and support (2018; checked Nov 2026)","3. Care Act 2014 statutory guidance: self-neglect (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: Dementia, and the Herbert Protocol (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK older people often live alone with family nearby. A night-time wanderer is rarely sent home alone the same night; delirium screening, social work and telecare are standard before discharge.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-18","n":1318,"prog":"Old Age Psychiatry","icon":"📚","title":"A house full of journals","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 14:00","where":"Older adult outpatient clinic","text":"Dr Leonard Okoro, a 67-year-old retired pathologist, has been referred by his GP after a home visit raised concerns about the state of his house. He has lived alone since his wife died eight years ago. He arrives on time, smartly dressed, and greets you as a colleague: 'Rather intrigued why I'm here, to be honest.'"},"task":"Take a history and carry out a mental state assessment, establishing whether this is hoarding disorder, screening for related conditions, and assessing the fire and other risks in his home.","first60":["Read the GP letter: what the GP saw, which rooms, any fire or fall, and that the daughter raised it.","Check his physical history and medication on the record; note any falls, eyesight problems or memory concerns.","Plan a curious, respectful tone. People who hoard often expect to be judged and close down fast.","Use his preferred title. A retired doctor may enjoy talking shop; let that build rapport.","Hold the structure: the collection, its impact, discarding and insight, other explanations, risk, then the mental state."],"ask":["'Tell me about life at home, in your words.'","'What do you collect? How did it start?'","'Roughly how many are there now, and where are they kept?'","'Which rooms can you still use for what they're meant for?'","'How would you feel if someone threw some of them away?'","'Have you ever had a fire, or tripped over anything?'","'Do you ever have to check, count or arrange things in a set way?'","'What do the journals mean to you? What would happen if they were taken?'","'How's your mood been since your wife died?'"],"recognise":["Hoarding disorder: persistent difficulty discarding, regardless of value, with build-up that clutters living areas and stops them being used. ICD-11 lists it separately from OCD.","Insight is often fair to poor: he may see the collection as valuable and the house as fine.","Bereavement is a common trigger; possessions can keep a lost person close.","OCD hoarding is driven by intrusive fears and feels unwanted; hoarding disorder feels wanted and pleasurable.","Late onset, new disinhibition or memory problems suggest a frontal or other dementia; psychosis shows as delusional reasons for keeping things.","Fire risk is the most urgent harm: blocked exits, paper near cookers, and broken smoke alarms."],"decide":[{"c":"Complete the history and mental state, explain hoarding disorder respectfully, agree a fire service Safe and Well visit and a home visit, and offer CBT for hoarding with his consent.","r":"Right: it names the problem without shame, tackles the urgent fire risk, and offers the treatment that works at a pace he can accept.","best":true},{"c":"Arrange for the council to clear the house next week for his safety.","r":"Wrong: forced clearance without his agreement is distressing, rarely lasts, and he has capacity to decide. Build motivation and reduce the immediate risks first.","best":false},{"c":"Prescribe an SSRI and discharge to the GP.","r":"Wrong: there is no depression or OCD, and medication is not first line for hoarding. He needs psychological treatment and a risk plan.","best":false}],"law":["He is presumed to have capacity. Unwise choices are not proof of incapacity; assess capacity for specific decisions, such as accepting help, if doubt arises.","Care Act 2014: self-neglect, including hoarding, can be a safeguarding concern. Seek his consent first; a local authority enquiry may proceed if risk is serious.","Confidentiality: ask before sharing details with his daughter; the fire service visit also needs his consent.","Environmental health powers exist for pests or serious hazards; use them only as a last resort."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"I'm not here to tell you to throw things away. I'd like to understand what the journals mean to you and how life at home is working."},{"to":"Patient","s":"What you describe has a name: hoarding disorder. It's common, it's nothing to be ashamed of, and it often starts after a loss. The first aim is simply to keep you safe."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Hoarding disorder with fair insight, onset after bereavement. No depression, OCD, psychosis or cognitive impairment on assessment. Urgent fire risk: paper in the kitchen, previous fire, smoke alarm disconnected. Fire service visit arranged with consent; referred for CBT for hoarding."}],"write":"Older adult clinic. Dr Leonard Okoro, 67M, retired pathologist, widowed 8 yrs, lives alone in a large house. Referred by GP after home visit; daughter concerned.\nHx: collects medical journals; began with late wife's psychiatry journals after her death, now >1,000 of several titles. Initially guarded about numbers. Piles in every room including kitchen; bedroom partly usable; spare rooms and dining room unusable. Small kitchen fire last year burnt ~20; no longer uses gas oven. Trips over stacks at night. Some dust and mice droppings. Distressed at the idea of discarding; still buys more online. Believes they may be valuable.\nNo checking, counting or contamination fears. No intrusive unwanted thoughts. Lifelong broad interests; no rigid routines or social difficulty reported. No delusional meaning attached.\nMood: 'fine'; sleep and appetite good; grief settled; no hopelessness or suicidal thoughts. Alcohol socially only; no drugs; non-smoker. No physical illness or medication.\nMSE: well kempt, warm, good rapport, slightly guarded at first. Speech normal. Mood 'fine', euthymic. No abnormal beliefs or perceptions. Cognition grossly intact (oriented, good recall). Insight fair: accepts fire risk, not that the quantity is a problem.\nRisk: fire (paper near cooker, smoke alarm disconnected), falls in the dark, blocked exits, pests; no self-harm or neglect of self-care.\nImpression: hoarding disorder, fair insight, linked to bereavement.\nPlan: fire service Safe and Well visit (consented); home visit with Clutter Image Rating; CBT for hoarding; ACE-III and bloods; family meeting with daughter if he agrees; GP review of falls and eyesight.","make":["Lead with curiosity, not concern. Let him talk about the journals with pride before you mention the house.","If he says 'enough', stay non-judgemental and come back to it; numbers matter.","Map the house room by room: which rooms work, which don't, and where the kitchen stands.","Separate hoarding from OCD, psychosis, depression, autism and dementia with one or two sharp questions each.","Ask specifically about fire: smoking, the cooker, smoke alarms, and blocked exits.","Make the MSE explicit: appearance, rapport, mood, beliefs, perceptions, cognition and insight."],"next":"The fire service fit new smoke alarms and help him clear the kitchen and hallway. He agrees to CBT for hoarding and, over six months, donates duplicate journals to a university medical library, which writes to thank him. His daughter joins two sessions. His memory testing is normal.","marks":["Built rapport with curiosity and respect before discussing the house.","Established what he collects, how many, since when, and the link to his wife.","Mapped the impact on each room, the kitchen fire and falls.","Explored difficulty discarding, ongoing acquiring and insight.","Screened for OCD, autism traits, psychosis, depression and grief, and cognitive decline.","Assessed fire and other risks and presented a clear mental state assessment."],"pitfalls":["Opening with 'Your house is a fire hazard' and losing him in the first minute.","Accepting 'enough' and never finding out the scale.","Calling it OCD without asking about intrusive thoughts or compulsions.","Skipping smoke alarms, the cooker and exits.","Forgetting cognition in a man whose hoarding began in later life."],"refs":["1. WHO ICD-11: 6B24 Hoarding disorder (checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE CG31 OCD and BDD (2005; checked Nov 2026)","3. Care Act 2014 statutory guidance: self-neglect (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: Hoarding disorder (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK fire and rescue services offer free home Safe and Well visits, and many areas have hoarding panels. Forced clearances are discouraged; services expect slow, consented work.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-29","n":1329,"prog":"Old Age Psychiatry","icon":"🧱","title":"Neighbours plotting against her at 74: assessing thought and perception","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 10:30, outpatient clinic","where":"Older adults community mental health team","text":"Mrs Dorothy Pennington, a 74-year-old retired dinner lady who lives alone, has been urgently referred after telling her son and daughter that her neighbours are trying to drive her out. She is irritated to be here and says it is the people next door who need looking at, not her. Her hearing aids are in her handbag."},"task":"Assess her mental state for abnormalities of thought and perception only, with a brief check of senses and cognition and of immediate risk. Do not take a full history.","first60":["Read the GP referral: what the family reported, duration, physical illnesses, medicines (steroids, dopamine agonists, anticholinergics), and any blood results.","Note sensory loss: ask her, kindly, to put her hearing aids in before you start, and check she has her glasses.","Expect irritation: you will not get far by arguing about the neighbours. Plan to listen and be curious.","Hold the map: the neighbours, other beliefs, hallucinations in each sense, thought interference, senses and cognition, risk.","Ask permission before any memory questions; frame them as routine for everyone her age."],"ask":["'Tell me what the neighbours have been doing, from the beginning.'","'When did you first realise? What happened at that moment?'","'Why do you think they're doing it? Is anyone else involved?'","'How do they get the smell into your house? Has anything been taken or tampered with?'","'Do you hear them? Where from, what do they say, and when?'","'Has your food or drink tasted strange? Seen anything odd, or felt anything on your skin?'","'Does anyone put thoughts in your head, take them away, or control what you do?'","'Have you thought about getting back at them, or about not going on?'"],"recognise":["Very-late-onset schizophrenia-like psychosis (VLOSLP): persecutory delusions centred on the home, often with a sudden meaningful onset.","Partition delusions: the belief that people, gas or sounds come through walls, ceilings or floors; typical in late-onset psychosis.","Third-person voices at night, plus olfactory and gustatory hallucinations: multimodal, all tied to the delusion.","Sensory impairment (unused hearing aids) and social isolation are known risk factors.","Intact orientation and recall make dementia or delirium less likely, but organic causes still need excluding.","Risk: conflict with neighbours, poor eating from fear of tampering, isolation, and retaliation."],"decide":[{"c":"Complete the thought and perception assessment, record the delusions and hallucinations, check cognition briefly, then arrange organic screen bloods and brain imaging, audiology review, and follow-up with the same clinician; discuss low-dose antipsychotic once trust is built.","r":"Right: it establishes the psychopathology, excludes organic causes, tackles the hearing deficit, and keeps her engaged.","best":true},{"c":"Tell her clearly that the neighbours are not doing it and that she is ill.","r":"Counterproductive: fixed beliefs do not yield to argument, and she will disengage. Acknowledge her distress and keep a neutral, curious stance.","best":false},{"c":"Diagnose Alzheimer's disease and start donepezil.","r":"Wrong: her orientation and recall are good. Psychosis in later life needs an organic screen, not an assumption of dementia.","best":false}],"law":["She has capacity and is an informal outpatient; she can decline treatment. Consider a Mental Health Act assessment only if risk to herself or others becomes serious.","Ask her consent before sharing details with her son and daughter; they can still give information to you.","If she is being harassed or the dispute escalates, safeguarding and the police may need to be involved; record what she reports."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Thank you for coming even though you think it's the neighbours who should be here. It sounds like you've had a very stressful year."},{"to":"Patient","s":"I can't see what's happening through that wall, but I can see how worn out it's made you. Let's work on how unsettled you feel at night."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Persecutory and partition delusions about neighbours for one year, with third-person voices at night and olfactory and gustatory hallucinations. Cognition grossly intact. Please arrange organic screen bloods; we will request MRI head and audiology."}],"write":"Older adults CMHT clinic. 74F, lives alone. Seen alone; hearing aids inserted at start.\nThought content: persecutory delusion that neighbours want her out of her house, ~1 yr, full conviction. Onset: saw them put up a satellite dish on the shared wall and 'knew' they wanted her house (likely delusional perception). Partition delusion: they pump a sweet smell through the bedroom wall. Believes they take her post. Delusion of food tampering. No grandiose, guilt or nihilistic ideas.\nPerception: 3rd-person voices of neighbours discussing her by name at night when quiet ('she won't last'), heard through the wall; olfactory (sweet gas smell); gustatory (tea tastes metallic). No visual or tactile.\nNo thought insertion, withdrawal or broadcast; no passivity.\nMood: angry, not depressed.\nSenses and cognition: hearing aids rarely worn; glasses. Orientated x3; 3-item recall 3/3.\nInsight: absent.\nRisk: shouted at neighbours, called police twice; sleeps in back room, towels under door; only eats sealed food; no intent to harm them or herself.\nImpression: very-late-onset schizophrenia-like psychosis (VLOSLP); exclude organic causes.\nPlan: bloods, MRI head, audiology; same-clinician follow-up 2 weeks; family meeting with consent.\nDr Chinonso CT2","make":["Acknowledge her irritation in your first sentence; never argue about the neighbours.","Ask how it began: 'What happened the moment you first knew?' finds the delusional perception.","Go sense by sense: hearing, smell, taste, sight, touch. Older people with late-onset psychosis often have several.","Ask how she responds: rooms she avoids, food she won't eat, calls to the police.","Ask her to put her hearing aids in; it is assessment and treatment at once.","A brief memory check is essential before you call it psychosis rather than dementia."],"next":"Her bloods and MRI are unremarkable apart from mild small vessel change. Audiology fits new hearing aids. After three visits from the same nurse she agrees to amisulpride 50 mg; at six weeks the voices are quieter and she sleeps in her own bedroom again, though she still distrusts the neighbours.","marks":["Acknowledged her annoyance and avoided arguing about the neighbours.","Elicited the persecutory belief: what, since when, why, who, and conviction.","Found the sudden onset and the partition delusion.","Screened hallucinations in every sense, with timing and her response.","Screened thought interference and passivity, and briefly checked mood.","Checked hearing, sight, orientation and recall, then asked about risk to herself and the neighbours."],"pitfalls":["Telling her the neighbours are not doing it.","Taking a full history instead of focusing on thought and perception.","Asking only about voices and missing smell and taste.","Leaving out sensory impairment.","Skipping a brief cognitive check in a 74-year-old."],"refs":["1. NICE CG178 Psychosis and schizophrenia in adults (2014; checked Nov 2026)","2. Howard R and colleagues: international consensus statement on psychosis starting in later life, Am J Psychiatry 2000","3. BNF: Amisulpride, risperidone (checked Nov 2026)","4. Royal College of Psychiatrists: Psychosis in older people (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: older adults services in the UK see many first presentations of psychosis after 65. Expect partition delusions and sensory loss. Building trust over several visits from the same clinician is normal practice and often matters more than speed.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-31","n":1331,"prog":"Old Age Psychiatry","icon":"💊","title":"Galantamine for Dad: answering his son's questions","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 14:20, memory clinic","where":"Memory clinic, community mental health centre","text":"Mr Harold Whitaker, a 79-year-old retired joiner, has just been told he has Alzheimer's disease of mild to moderate severity, and the team have agreed to start galantamine. He takes bisoprolol and his pulse today is 58. His son, Mr Neil Whitaker, a 50-year-old teacher, asks to speak with you about the medicine. Harold has said he is happy for you to talk to Neil."},"task":"Explain to Neil, Harold's son, the benefits and side effects of galantamine, how it is started and monitored, and answer his concerns. A history is not needed.","first60":["Confirm Harold's consent for you to discuss his treatment with Neil, and note it in the record.","Check the clinic notes: diagnosis, severity, today's cognitive score, pulse, any ECG, and the full drug list (bisoprolol matters).","Have a galantamine leaflet ready, plus the Alzheimer's Society and carers' support details.","Plan the order: what he knows, the diagnosis in a line, how it works, benefits, dosing, side effects, monitoring, his worries, close.","Expect three worries: does it work, is it safe for Dad's heart, and what else can be done."],"ask":["'What do you already know about galantamine, and what's on your mind about it?'","'What have you noticed in your dad over the last year?'","'What have you read about these medicines?'","'Does your dad have any heart, chest or stomach problems that you know of?'","'Who helps him with his tablets at the moment?'","'How are you managing yourself, with work and looking after him?'","'What would make the biggest difference for him at home right now?'"],"recognise":["Galantamine is one of three cholinesterase inhibitors (with donepezil and rivastigmine) recommended by NICE for mild to moderate Alzheimer's disease.","It treats symptoms only: it can steady or modestly lift memory, function and behaviour for a time, but it does not stop the disease.","Side effects come mostly from extra acetylcholine: nausea, vomiting, diarrhoea, poor appetite, weight loss, dizziness, a slow pulse and fainting.","A beta-blocker plus a cholinesterase inhibitor can slow the heart further: a pulse in the 50s needs an ECG and a closer watch.","A carer who asks 'does it actually work?' wants honesty, not a sales pitch; overselling loses trust later.","Driving after a dementia diagnosis is a separate safety issue: the licence holder must tell the DVLA."],"decide":[{"c":"Give honest, balanced information on benefits and side effects, arrange an ECG before the first dose because of bisoprolol and a pulse of 58, start galantamine XL 8 mg with breakfast, and book a review with a contact number.","r":"Right: it answers his questions truthfully, handles the heart risk properly and leaves a safe plan with a named contact.","best":true},{"c":"Reassure Neil that the tablet will stop his father getting worse.","r":"Wrong: galantamine does not halt the disease. False hope damages trust when decline continues.","best":false},{"c":"Decide not to prescribe because Harold takes a beta-blocker.","r":"Too cautious: a beta-blocker is a reason to check an ECG and monitor the pulse, not an automatic bar. Many people on both do well.","best":false}],"law":["Confidentiality: Harold has capacity and has agreed to Neil being told; record his consent and its scope.","Mental Capacity Act 2005: presume Harold has capacity to decide about treatment; Neil supports him but does not decide for him.","Lasting Power of Attorney: encourage Harold to make health and finance LPAs while he can.","DVLA: a person diagnosed with dementia must tell the DVLA; the doctor advises this and records it."],"say":[{"to":"Son","s":"I'll be straight with you, Neil: this isn't a cure. For some people it steadies memory and day-to-day life for a while, and we'll know within a few months if it's helping your dad."},{"to":"Son","s":"Because of his bisoprolol and a pulse of 58, we'll do a heart tracing before he starts, and his GP will check his pulse at each visit."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Started galantamine XL 8 mg each morning with food; increase to 16 mg after 4 weeks if tolerated. On bisoprolol, pulse 58, ECG done. Please check pulse and weight at each visit; hold and contact us if pulse below 50, fainting or falls."}],"write":"Memory clinic. Discussion with son Neil Whitaker (50), with Harold's consent.\nHarold Whitaker, 79M, Alzheimer's disease, mild to moderate. On bisoprolol 2.5 mg. Pulse 58 today.\nNeil's concerns: does it work; safety with heart; liver (read online); addictive; cost; daffodil supplements; what else can be done.\nExplained: cholinesterase inhibitor, raises acetylcholine; symptomatic, not a cure; realistic benefit: some improve, many stay steadier for a while, some no change; long-term if helping.\nDosing: galantamine XL 8 mg with breakfast for 4 weeks, then 16 mg; max 24 mg. Restart at 8 mg if missed for more than a few days.\nSide effects: nausea, poor appetite, weight loss, diarrhoea, dizziness, slow pulse, fainting; rare serious rash (stop, seek help). Not addictive. Free on NHS. Liver: not a usual problem.\nPlan: ECG before first dose. GP to check pulse and weight. Review in memory clinic 3 months then 6-monthly. Leaflet, Alzheimer's Society, carers' assessment, LPA and DVLA advice given. Contact number given.\nNeil summarised plan accurately.\nDr N. Chinonso, CT2","make":["Ask what he knows and fears first; his questions set the order of your explanation.","Give honest numbers and a clear review point: 'we'll judge it at three months'.","Link side effects to action: what to watch for, and what to do if it happens.","Name the heart issue yourself before he has to ask, and say what you will check.","Answer the surprise questions calmly: addiction, cost, liver, daffodils.","Widen out at the end: the tablet is one part of the plan, not all of it."],"next":"Harold's ECG shows sinus rhythm at 56 with a normal PR interval. He starts galantamine XL 8 mg; mild nausea settles in a week. At 16 mg his pulse stays above 52. At three months Neil says his father is calmer and repeats himself less. Harold has told the DVLA and is having a driving assessment.","marks":["Confirmed Harold's consent and asked what Neil already knew and feared.","Explained how galantamine works in plain words and that it is not a cure.","Gave honest, realistic expectations of benefit and when it would be judged.","Explained dosing: XL once daily with food, step up after four weeks.","Described common and serious side effects and what to do about them.","Addressed the heart, liver, addiction, cost and supplement questions, then closed with review, leaflet and contacts."],"pitfalls":["Promising that the medicine will stop the decline.","Missing the bisoprolol and slow pulse, or failing to plan an ECG.","Reciting a list of side effects with no advice on what to do.","Taking a history from Neil when the task is to explain.","Ending without a review date, a contact number or carer support."],"refs":["1. NICE NG97 Dementia: assessment, management and support (2018; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE TA217 Donepezil, galantamine, rivastigmine and memantine for Alzheimer's disease (checked Nov 2026)","3. BNF: Galantamine (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: Alzheimer's disease, treatment (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, the memory clinic starts the drug and the GP often continues it under shared care. Families expect frank talk about limits; 'it may help for a while' is respected far more than optimism. Carers have a legal right to a carers' assessment.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-34","n":1334,"prog":"Core: first weeks and on call","icon":"😠","title":"Angry mother: clozapine fears and taking him home","src":"","moment":{"when":"Thursday 16:00, ward doctor","where":"Family room, adult acute ward","text":"Mr Jamal Hussain, aged 34, has treatment-resistant schizophrenia and has been on the ward for three months. Clozapine started 12 weeks ago; he is improving slowly but still needs prompting to wash and change. Before admission he lived with his older brother, who drinks heavily, in a neglected flat, and the brother seems to have taken his money. His liver tests were abnormal on arrival but all blood results since have been normal. His mother, Mrs Shabnam Hussain, 58, arrives with printouts saying clozapine damages bone marrow. She wants to take him home, 30 miles away, where she lives with her husband, who also drinks heavily. Jamal has agreed she can be involved."},"task":"Meet Mrs Hussain, calm her anger, and address her concerns: clozapine and blood monitoring, his slow progress and care on the ward, his earlier blood tests, and her wish to take him home. Explain how to complain if she wishes.","first60":["Check Jamal's consent to share information with his mother, and its limits, in the notes.","Look at his clozapine record: start date, current dose, every FBC result and its status, plasma level, LFTs then and now.","Read the latest nursing and OT notes so you can describe his care and progress honestly.","Find a private room, offer a seat and a drink, and ask a nurse who knows Jamal to join if possible.","Plan to listen fully first; don't defend the team until she has said everything."],"ask":["'Please tell me what's worrying you most, and we'll go through each thing.'","'What did you read about clozapine?'","'What have you noticed when you visit?'","'What would you like to happen?'","'Tell me about home: who would be there with Jamal, and how is everyone?'","'How did Jamal get on living with his brother?'","'How are you coping with all this yourself?'"],"recognise":["Anger in a relative is usually fear and helplessness: she has watched her son struggle and feels shut out.","Her internet fact is partly right: clozapine can cause a dangerous fall in white cells, which is exactly why blood monitoring is compulsory.","Clozapine works slowly; in treatment-resistant illness gains often build over months, so three months is early.","The abnormal liver tests on admission have settled; likely linked to life at the brother's flat, now monitored routinely.","Home with a husband who drinks heavily, 30 miles away, while self-care is still poor, is a weak discharge plan right now.","The brother taking his money is financial abuse: a safeguarding issue, not just family history."],"decide":[{"c":"Listen fully, acknowledge her anger and any lapses in communication, explain clozapine monitoring and his results, explain why discharge home now is not safe, offer a family meeting with the consultant, and give her the complaints route.","r":"Right: it respects her role, corrects fear with facts, protects Jamal, and keeps her as a partner in his care.","best":true},{"c":"Reassure her that clozapine is completely safe and that she has nothing to worry about.","r":"Wrong: it isn't true and she knows it. Honest risk plus how we monitor it builds far more trust.","best":false},{"c":"Agree to discharge him to her home this week to defuse the situation.","r":"Unsafe: he still needs prompting with self-care, clozapine bloods must be set up locally, and home has its own risks. Discharge is planned, not traded for calm.","best":false}],"law":["Confidentiality: Jamal has agreed to his mother's involvement; share what he has agreed to and record it.","If Jamal is detained, his nearest relative can order discharge with 72 hours' written notice, which the responsible clinician can bar if he would be dangerous to himself or others (MHA s23 and s25).","Care Act 2014 section 42: suspected financial abuse by his brother needs a safeguarding concern to the local authority.","NHS complaints: she can raise concerns through PALS or make a formal complaint to the trust; tell her how, and that it will not affect Jamal's care."],"say":[{"to":"Mother","s":"You're right that clozapine can affect the white blood cells. So his blood is checked weekly, and we'd stop it straight away if the count fell. All his results have been normal."},{"to":"Mother","s":"I'm sorry nobody has rung you. That should have happened. I'll make sure his named nurse calls you every week from now on."},{"to":"Consultant","s":"Mrs Hussain is angry about clozapine and wants Jamal home. I've explained monitoring and results. She'd like a family meeting this week, and she may complain. Please note: brother took his money; safeguarding concern raised; husband drinks heavily."}],"write":"Meeting with Mrs Shabnam Hussain (mother, 58), Jamal's consent recorded. Staff nurse present.\nJamal Hussain, 34M, TRS, admitted 3 months. Clozapine started 12 weeks ago, current 300 mg/day; FBCs weekly, all normal. LFTs raised on admission, normal on every test since.\nConcerns: clozapine 'destroys bone marrow' (internet); feels he's experimented on; looks unkempt; no phone calls from staff; wants him home now.\nExplained: rationale for clozapine (two previous antipsychotics ineffective); neutropenia risk and mandatory FBC monitoring (weekly to 18 wks, fortnightly to 1 yr, then 4-weekly); his results; slow response expected over months; LFTs now normal; self-care being supported by OT and nursing.\nHome: husband drinks heavily, 30 miles away, no local clozapine clinic yet. Advised discharge now would be unsafe; graded leave plan instead. Supported accommodation also an option.\nBrother: took Jamal's benefits. Safeguarding concern raised today.\nApologised for lack of contact; named nurse to call weekly. PALS and complaints leaflet given; she intends to complain, supported.\nPlan: family meeting with consultant Monday 2pm; carer's assessment referral; clozapine leaflet given.\nDr N. Chinonso, CT2","make":["Let her finish. Every interruption restarts her anger.","Agree with what is true before you correct what isn't: 'You're right that clozapine can affect the blood.'","Use his real results, not general reassurance.","Apologise specifically for what went wrong, and fix it in front of her.","Be honest about home: name the risks kindly, and offer a plan rather than a flat no.","Never discourage a complaint; hand her the route."],"next":"At the family meeting the consultant agrees a graded leave plan, first day visits, then a night at her home once clozapine bloods are arranged locally. Mrs Hussain still submits a complaint about communication; the ward manager meets her and introduces weekly calls. The safeguarding enquiry moves Jamal's benefits to an appointee. By month six he washes without prompting most days.","marks":["Stayed calm, let her speak, and acknowledged her anger and worry.","Explained why clozapine was chosen and why progress is slow.","Explained the white cell risk honestly and how monitoring protects him, with his results.","Explained the earlier liver results and that they are now normal.","Discussed discharge home honestly, including her husband's drinking and the brother's financial abuse.","Apologised for poor contact, offered a family meeting and explained how to complain."],"pitfalls":["Becoming defensive or arguing about her internet sources.","Saying clozapine is 'perfectly safe'.","Promising discharge, or refusing it flatly with no plan.","Missing the safeguarding issue about the brother's money.","Discouraging her complaint or forgetting PALS."],"refs":["1. NICE CG178 Psychosis and schizophrenia in adults (2014; checked Nov 2026)","2. BNF and SPC: Clozapine, blood monitoring requirements (checked Nov 2026)","3. MHRA Drug Safety Update: clozapine and gastrointestinal hypomotility (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS England: How to complain to the NHS; PALS (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in UK services, relatives' complaints are a right, not an insult. Doctors are expected to give the route to complain openly. A specific apology ('nobody rang you, and that was wrong') is not an admission of liability and is encouraged by the duty of candour.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-38","n":1338,"prog":"Old Age Psychiatry","icon":"🌱","title":"A stone lighter since retiring: depression, its physical mimics and risk at a home visit","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 11:00, home visit","where":"Community mental health team for older people, the patient's home","text":"Mr Reginald Thompson, 65, retired as a bus driver last year. His GP has referred him because of weight loss and low mood. You are the core trainee in the older people's team. His wife, Pat, lets you in. Reg is in his armchair in a dressing gown at 11 a.m., the curtains half drawn and the television off."},"task":"Carry out a focused history and mental state assessment to reach a diagnosis: depressive symptoms and severity, physical causes of weight loss, alcohol, a brief cognitive check, psychotic features, background stressors and risk, then summarise and involve his wife.","first60":["Read the GP referral: how much weight, over how long, which bloods were done, current medicines, past history and smoking.","Observe as you come in: self-care, the room, slowness of movement and speech, eye contact. This is your mental state assessment starting.","Ask Reg if he is happy for Pat to stay; you may want a few minutes alone later for risk questions.","Expect slow, short answers; plan to move from open questions to simple closed ones.","Keep the essentials in mind: depression severity, a physical cause, alcohol, psychosis, cognition and suicide risk."],"ask":["'How have things been for you lately?'","'Is your mood low most of the day, every day? Are mornings or evenings worse?'","'What do you still enjoy? What about the allotment?'","'How much weight have you lost? Any pain, bowel change, cough, or blood anywhere?'","'How much do you drink in a typical week?'","'You mentioned money. If you look at what you've got coming in, what does that tell you?'","'Do you ever feel you'd be better off dead? Have you thought how?'","'Are you still driving?'"],"recognise":["Early waking with dread, worse mornings, slowness and weight loss are biological features that point to a more severe depression.","In later life, depression often presents with weight loss, physical complaints or poor memory rather than sadness.","Weight loss of a stone in 3 months needs a physical cause excluded: cancer, thyroid, diabetes, alcohol, medicines.","A money worry that shifts with evidence is worry; one held with certainty against clear evidence is a delusion of poverty and means psychotic depression.","'Don't know' answers given slowly, with effort and right answers when encouraged, suggest depression rather than dementia.","Older men who live with pain, loss of role, alcohol and passive death wishes carry a high suicide risk even without a plan."],"decide":[{"c":"Diagnose a severe depressive episode without psychotic features, ask the GP to complete the physical work-up, start mirtazapine with sodium monitoring, arrange weekly care coordinator contact, and agree a safety plan with Reg and Pat.","r":"Right: it treats a serious but non-psychotic depression at home, keeps the physical cause question open, and builds safety around him.","best":true},{"c":"Refer for a memory clinic assessment because he is slow and forgetful.","r":"Wrong order: his slowness and poor concentration fit depression. Treat the depression first and recheck cognition when mood improves.","best":false},{"c":"Reassure him that feeling low after retirement is normal and suggest a hobby.","r":"Unsafe: it misses a severe depressive episode with weight loss and passive death wishes, and any physical cause.","best":false}],"law":["He has capacity to consent to the visit and to treatment; check he agrees to Pat being present and to sharing information with her.","If psychotic features appear, he stops eating or drinking, or suicide risk rises, discuss urgent admission or home treatment with the consultant; consider the Mental Health Act only if he refuses and risk is high.","Driving: advise not to drive while concentration is poor; with severe depression and significant concentration problems or suicidal thoughts, he must tell the DVLA.","Safeguarding: ask about medicines stockpiles and any firearms or shotgun certificates in the house."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Reg, what you're describing sounds like depression, and quite a heavy one. It's an illness, not a weakness, and it's very treatable at your age."},{"to":"Wife","s":"Pat, you know him best. If he stops eating or drinking, talks about ending his life, or says things that seem untrue, call us the same day."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Severe depressive episode without psychotic features. Weight loss ~6 kg in 3 months: please complete physical work-up. Starting mirtazapine 15 mg nocte; please check sodium at 2-4 weeks. Passive death wishes, no plan. Weekly CMHT contact."}],"write":"Home visit, CMHT older people. Reginald (Reg) Thompson, 65M, retired bus driver. Lives with wife Pat. GP referral: weight loss, low mood.\nHx: 4-5 months low mood since retirement, worse mornings, wakes 4am with dread. No enjoyment (stopped allotment), low energy, poor concentration, feels useless and a burden. Appetite poor; lost ~1 stone (6 kg) in 3 months. No pain, bowel change, cough, bleeding, urinary symptoms. Ex-smoker. GP bloods 2 wks ago 'fine' (full list not seen). Meds: amlodipine 5 mg.\nAlcohol: 2 cans bitter most nights (~25 units/wk), more since retiring. No other drugs.\nPast: no previous episodes. FH: mother 'bad nerves'. Stressors: loss of role; best friend died last year.\nMSE: dressing gown at 11am, slow movements, long pauses, quiet monotonous speech, poor eye contact. Mood low, affect flat. Money worries (pension smaller): shifts when reviewed, 'I know we're alright really'; not delusional. No nihilistic or hypochondriacal delusions; no hallucinations. Brief cognition: oriented, 3/3 recall with effort. Insight partial.\nRisk: passive wish to die, no plan or intent, no previous attempts. Protective: Pat, grandchildren. Eating little, drinking fluids. No firearms. Drives to shops, not after drinking.\nImpression: severe depressive episode without psychotic symptoms; physical cause for weight loss not yet excluded.\nPlan: diagnosis explained to Reg and Pat. Mirtazapine 15 mg nocte. GP: physical work-up, sodium 2-4 wks. Not to drive for now. Alcohol reduction. Care coordinator weekly. Consultant review next week. Crisis plan given. Pat: carer's assessment.\nDr Chinonso CT2","make":["Watch before you ask: dressing gown at 11, slowness and pauses are mental state findings to record.","Switch to short closed questions once you see open ones are too hard for him.","Ask about weight in numbers and over what time, then the red flags of a physical cause.","Test the money worry against facts before calling it a worry or a delusion.","Ask about death wishes directly, then plan, means, firearms and stockpiles.","Name the diagnosis and its seriousness to both of them, with hope."],"next":"The GP's repeat bloods and chest X-ray are normal. Reg starts mirtazapine; his sodium is normal at three weeks. By six weeks he sleeps until 6, has regained 2 kg, and goes to the allotment with Pat twice a week. He starts behavioural activation with the older adults' psychological therapies team.","marks":["Adapted questions to his slowness and validated his distress.","Elicited depressive symptoms with duration, diurnal variation and severity.","Explored physical causes of weight loss, medicines and alcohol.","Did a brief cognitive check and separated depression from dementia.","Tested money worries against evidence and screened for psychotic features.","Assessed suicide risk, self-neglect and driving, named depression and involved his wife."],"pitfalls":["Long open questions he cannot answer, followed by giving up on the history.","Assuming weight loss is just the depression without asking for red flags or GP tests.","Calling a money worry 'understandable' without testing whether it is fixed.","Missing alcohol, driving and firearms in the risk assessment.","Labelling slowness and forgetfulness as dementia."],"refs":["1. NICE NG222: adult depression guideline (2022; checked Nov 2026)","2. BNF: Mirtazapine (checked Nov 2026)","3. DVLA: Assessing fitness to drive, psychiatric disorders (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: Depression in older people (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: home visits are routine in UK older people's teams. Ask before you sit, accept tea, and use what you see as evidence. By law, a carer can ask the council to assess her own needs as a carer; offering this is part of the plan.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-40","n":1340,"prog":"Core: first weeks and on call","icon":"🧠","title":"Ignoring his left side after a stroke: a bedside parietal lobe check","src":"","moment":{"when":"Friday 14:00, liaison psychiatry","where":"Stroke rehabilitation ward","text":"Mr Harold Fenwick, 68, right-handed, had a stroke three weeks ago leaving weakness of his left arm and leg. His family feel his thinking has changed too: he ignores people on his left and struggles to dress. The ward team has asked psychiatry to assess his parietal lobe function. He is sitting in a chair by the bed, his lunch tray with the left half untouched."},"task":"Carry out a bedside assessment of parietal lobe function: neglect and inattention, constructional and dressing apraxia, awareness of his weakness, cortical sensation, dominant parietal signs, visual fields and praxis, then explain the findings and report to the team.","first60":["Read the stroke notes: scan report (side and territory), date, weakness, any aphasia, and therapy notes on dressing and transfers.","Prepare your kit: paper, pen, a ruler-drawn line, a sheet of scattered letters or stars, a coin, a key and a hospital gown.","Sit to his right first so he can see you; later you will move stimuli into his left.","Check glasses and hearing aids, pain, and whether he needs the toilet; ask a nurse to stay as a chaperone for the touch tests.","Plan your order and keep each instruction to one short sentence."],"ask":["'Which hand do you write with?'","'Have you noticed anything different about how you see or notice things since the stroke?'","'Look at my nose. Tell me which finger moves: this one, this one, or both?'","'Please mark the middle of this line.'","'Copy these two shapes as carefully as you can.'","'Is anything wrong with your left arm?'","'Eyes closed. What am I putting in your hand?'","'Put your right hand on your left ear.'"],"recognise":["Left weakness in a right-handed man means a right (non-dominant) hemisphere stroke: expect left neglect, constructional and dressing apraxia, and unawareness of the weakness.","Missing the left finger only when both move (extinction) is a sign of inattention; missing it even alone is a field defect or primary sensory loss.","A field defect alone is usually noticed and compensated by turning the head; neglect is not.","Cortical sensory loss can only be called if basic touch is intact in that hand.","Finger agnosia, left-right confusion, acalculia and agraphia (Gerstmann features) point to the dominant (left) parietal lobe; expect them to be normal here.","A left inferior quadrantanopia fits damage to the upper fibres of the optic radiation in the right parietal lobe."],"decide":[{"c":"Complete a structured bedside assessment, record each finding, explain right parietal damage plainly, and advise the team on neglect strategies, OT dressing work, falls risk and driving.","r":"Right: it answers the referral question, guides rehabilitation, and keeps him safe.","best":true},{"c":"Do an MMSE and report that his cognition is fine because he scores 27.","r":"Wrong: global screens largely miss neglect, apraxia and cortical sensory loss, the very problems his family describe.","best":false},{"c":"Diagnose depression because he seems uninterested in his left side and his family.","r":"Wrong: unawareness of his weakness and ignoring one side are neurological signs. Screen mood separately; do not mistake neglect for apathy.","best":false}],"law":["Consent: he has capacity for a bedside assessment; explain each test and stop if he declines.","Chaperone: offer one for any touch, and record whether it was accepted.","Capacity: unawareness of deficits can affect decisions such as going home alone; assess capacity for that decision separately if it arises.","Driving: he must not drive for at least a month after a stroke; a persisting field defect or neglect must be reported to the DVLA and will usually bar driving."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"The stroke has affected the part of the brain on the right side that helps you notice things on your left and judge space. That's why the left can seem to disappear."},{"to":"Family","s":"When you visit, start on his right, then move gently to his left and encourage him to turn. Put things he needs on his right for now."},{"to":"Ward team (note)","s":"Right parietal syndrome: left visual and sensory inattention, left neglect on line bisection and cancellation, constructional and dressing apraxia, partial anosognosia, left astereognosis and agraphaesthesia, left inferior quadrantanopia. Gerstmann features absent. Please refer to OT for neglect and dressing work."}],"write":"Liaison psychiatry, stroke rehab ward. Harold (Harry) Fenwick, 68M, right-handed. Stroke 3/52 ago, L hemiparesis. Referral: parietal lobe function. Consent given; nurse chaperone present; glasses on; no pain.\nInattention: single finger L and R seen; on simultaneous stimulation misses L (visual extinction). Touch: feels L and R singly, misses L when both touched (sensory extinction).\nNeglect: line bisection mark well right of centre; cancellation: misses most targets on L half. Ate only R half of lunch.\nConstructional: intersecting pentagons, L side distorted, no overlap; clock numbers crowded on R, L side empty.\nDressing: gown on R arm only, unaware L sleeve hanging.\nAnosognosia: says L arm 'just a bit lazy', thinks he could walk home today.\nCortical sensation (primary touch intact both hands): coin and key named in R hand, not L; numbers drawn on L palm not identified; two-point and position sense impaired L, normal R.\nDominant parietal: finger naming, L-R orientation, calculation (100-7 x3, 38-14) and writing a sentence all normal; writing drifts to R of page.\nFields: L inferior quadrantanopia both eyes.\nPraxis: ideomotor (mime brushing teeth, salute) and ideational (cap pen, write) normal with R hand.\nRepetition normal; digit span 6 forwards.\nImpression: right (non-dominant) parietal syndrome. Dominant parietal function intact.\nPlan: findings explained to Harry; family guidance; OT neglect and dressing programme; falls risk flagged; no driving, DVLA; mood screen next visit.\nDr Chinonso CT2","make":["Do the quick setup that prevents false findings: glasses, hearing, handedness, pain, chaperone.","Test each side alone before both together: only then does a miss mean extinction.","Check basic touch before calling any cortical sensory loss.","Give a reason for each task in one line; it turns 'what's the point?' into cooperation.","Test dominant signs even when you expect them normal: a normal result is a finding.","Close by naming what you found in plain words and what the team will do."],"next":"OT starts a scanning programme and dressing practice, and the ward put his tray and call bell on the left with prompts to turn. Three weeks later he dresses with one verbal cue and notices visitors on his left. He accepts that he cannot drive for now.","marks":["Introduced self, gave the reason, consent, handedness, glasses, pain and chaperone.","Tested visual and sensory inattention correctly, singly then together, plus line bisection or cancellation.","Tested constructional and dressing apraxia and his awareness of the weakness.","Tested cortical sensation after confirming basic touch.","Tested Gerstmann features, fields by quadrant, and praxis.","Summarised the findings as right parietal and closed with a plan."],"pitfalls":["Using a global screen alone and missing neglect and apraxia.","Testing extinction without first testing each side alone.","Calling astereognosis in a hand with no basic touch.","Arguing with him when he denies the weakness.","Long, multi-part instructions he cannot follow."],"refs":["1. NICE NG236 Stroke rehabilitation in adults (2023; checked Nov 2026)","2. Royal College of Physicians: National clinical guideline for stroke (2023; checked Nov 2026)","3. DVLA: Assessing fitness to drive, neurological disorders (checked Nov 2026)","4. Stroke Association: Visual neglect after stroke (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK stroke units rely on occupational therapists for neglect and dressing rehabilitation; your clear, test-by-test note helps them plan. Write what he did (for example 'mark 3 cm right of centre'), not just 'neglect present'.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-43","n":1343,"prog":"Core: first weeks and on call","icon":"🌡️","title":"High fever and stiffness after haloperidol: explaining NMS to his father","src":"","moment":{"when":"Friday 16:30","where":"Adult acute admissions ward, relatives' room","text":"Kofi Asante, 18, admitted six days ago with a first episode of psychosis, started haloperidol 5 mg three times daily three days ago and has since had two doses of 10 mg intramuscularly for agitation. Nurses now find temperature 41°C, pulse 104, BP 140/90, respiratory rate 22 and rigid muscles. WCC 14, CK 1024 IU/L, AST 44, ALT 82; urine normal. The medical registrar is on the way. His father, Kwame, has just arrived on the ward. Kofi has agreed you may speak to him."},"task":"Explain to Mr Asante what is causing his son's illness, set out the management plan, and address his concerns. A history is not needed.","first60":["Make sure the emergency is in hand first: haloperidol stopped and crossed off the chart, observations every 15 minutes, medical registrar called, transfer being arranged.","Check the facts you will share: doses and times of haloperidol, the observations, the bloods, and that urine and admission bloods were normal.","Confirm Kofi's consent to share and how much he wants his father told.","Find a private room; ask a nurse to come with you if possible.","Expect anger. Plan to explain honestly, apologise for what has happened, and describe what is being done now."],"ask":["'What have the staff told you so far?'","'What's worrying you most right now?'","'Is there anything you'd like me to go over again?'","'Would it help to write down the main points?'","'Would you like to be with Kofi when he moves to the medical ward?'","'Would you like a meeting with his consultant about what happened?'"],"recognise":["Fever, rigidity, fast pulse, raised BP and breathing rate after a recent rise in a high-potency antipsychotic: neuroleptic malignant syndrome until proven otherwise.","CK above 1,000 with raised white cells and liver enzymes fits muscle breakdown in NMS.","Risk factors here: high-potency drug, rapid dose increase, intramuscular doses, agitation and probably dehydration; young men are affected more often.","Other causes must be ruled out in parallel: infection including meningitis or encephalitis, serotonin syndrome, catatonia, and stimulant drugs.","The father's anger is fear. He needs honesty, a plan, and to know someone will be accountable."],"decide":[{"c":"Stop all antipsychotics, transfer Kofi urgently to the acute medical team for fluids, cooling, monitoring and specialist treatment, explain this honestly to his father, apologise, and arrange a consultant meeting and duty-of-candour follow-up.","r":"Right: NMS is a medical emergency, and being open with the family is both a legal duty and the best way to keep their trust.","best":true},{"c":"Keep Kofi on the psychiatric ward with paracetamol and fans, and restart haloperidol at a lower dose tomorrow.","r":"Dangerous: NMS needs medical-level monitoring and fluids, and continuing the same drug can be fatal.","best":false},{"c":"Tell his father it is probably a virus and avoid discussing the medication until the diagnosis is certain.","r":"Wrong: misleading a family breaches the duty of candour, and he will learn the truth later.","best":false}],"law":["Duty of candour (Health and Social Care Act 2008, Regulation 20): tell the family, with Kofi's consent, apologise, and follow up in writing; the trust must report the incident.","Confidentiality: share only what Kofi has agreed; if he loses capacity, share what his father needs to support him in his best interests.","If Kofi refuses medical treatment, assess his capacity for that decision; treatment of a physical illness is under the MCA 2005, not the Mental Health Act.","Report the reaction to the MHRA on a Yellow Card."],"say":[{"to":"Father","s":"I'm very sorry this has happened. Kofi has had a rare but serious reaction to his antipsychotic medicine, and we've stopped it and are moving him to the medical team now."},{"to":"Father","s":"It isn't an allergy. It's an unpredictable reaction that can happen even at ordinary doses, but the higher doses and injections made it more likely."},{"to":"Medical registrar (handover)","s":"18-year-old man, suspected NMS: temp 41, HR 104, BP 140/90, RR 22, lead-pipe rigidity, CK 1024, WCC 14, ALT 82. Haloperidol stopped. Needs IV fluids, cooling, sepsis screen, U&Es and CK repeat, and consideration of HDU."}],"write":"Ward doctor note, 16:50. Met Mr Kwame Asante (father) with Kofi's consent; staff nurse present.\nExplained: suspected neuroleptic malignant syndrome (temp 41, HR 104, BP 140/90, RR 22, rigidity, CK 1024, WCC 14, AST 44, ALT 82) after haloperidol 5 mg TDS and 2 x 10 mg IM. Not an allergy; rare, unpredictable, more likely with high-potency drugs, rapid increases and injections.\nPlan explained: all antipsychotics stopped; urgent transfer to acute medical team; IV fluids, cooling, paracetamol, lorazepam if needed; further treatment (dantrolene or bromocriptine) as physicians decide; sepsis screen to exclude infection; CK, U&Es, LFTs daily. Complications explained (kidney injury, chest infection, clots). Most recover in 1-2 weeks. Honest that it can be life-threatening.\nFuture: no antipsychotic for at least 2 weeks after full recovery; then a different antipsychotic at low dose, slow increase, CK and temperature checks; recorded as adverse reaction.\nConcerns: angry about injections; apology given; consultant meeting Monday; duty of candour; incident form completed; PALS details given.\nMr Asante repeated plan accurately. Will accompany Kofi to medical ward.\nDr Chinonso, CT1","make":["Start with honesty and an apology; it is the fastest way through anger.","Use the numbers sparingly: one sentence on fever, pulse, stiffness and the muscle enzyme is enough.","Say clearly that it is not an allergy and not caused by anything the family did.","Give a concrete plan: who, where, what tonight, what tomorrow.","Answer 'could he die?' truthfully: serious, but most people recover with prompt treatment.","Explain how his psychosis will be treated later, so the father is not left fearing no treatment at all."],"next":"Kofi spends four days on the acute medical unit with IV fluids, cooling and lorazepam; his CK peaks at 3,800 then falls, and his kidneys stay normal. Two weeks after full recovery, aripiprazole is started at 5 mg with daily temperature and weekly CK checks. Mr Asante meets the consultant, receives a written apology, and joins the family sessions run by the early intervention team.","marks":["Opened honestly, with Kofi's consent, and gave an early apology.","Explained the findings and test results in plain language.","Named NMS, said it is not an allergy, and explained the risk factors.","Described the immediate plan, including medical transfer and monitoring.","Explained complications and outlook honestly.","Addressed anger, blame and complaint routes, and set out future antipsychotic treatment."],"pitfalls":["Calling it an allergy, or blaming 'his illness'.","Overloading him with numbers and jargon such as 'rhabdomyolysis'.","Promising he will be fine, or avoiding the question about death.","Getting defensive about rapid tranquillisation.","Saying he can never have antipsychotics again."],"refs":["1. Taylor DM, Barnes TRE, Young AH. Prescribing Guidelines in Psychiatry, 15th edition: neuroleptic malignant syndrome (checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG10 Violence and aggression guideline (2015; checked Nov 2026)","3. CQC. Regulation 20: Duty of candour (checked Nov 2026)","4. BNF: antipsychotic drugs, neuroleptic malignant syndrome (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK services expect you to tell families promptly when treatment causes harm and to say 'I'm sorry' without waiting for blame to be decided. An apology is not an admission of liability.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-51","n":1351,"prog":"Old Age Psychiatry","icon":"🦊","title":"Lewy body dementia: Gerald's grandson wants to know what can be done","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 14:00, memory clinic","where":"Older adults memory clinic, community mental health base","text":"Gerald Okafor, an 82-year-old widower, has had worsening memory for 18 months, then stiffness, slowness and frequent falls for nine months. He talks warmly to his late wife and his own mother as if they are in the room, and sees a cat, a fox and children's faces around the house. A DaT scan supports dementia with Lewy bodies and he takes rivastigmine 3 mg twice daily. Things are worse at home. His grandson Ellis, 29, has come in, and says, 'We're worried sick.'"},"task":"Speak with Ellis about his grandfather's behaviour, check for a new cause of the decline, explain dementia with Lewy bodies and the scan, go through the treatment options honestly, and address his ideas, concerns and expectations.","first60":["Check the letter or record: Gerald's written consent to share information with Ellis, and who holds any lasting power of attorney.","Read the DaT scan report, the date rivastigmine started, the last pulse and ECG, and the full medicine list from the GP record.","Look for anything new: recent infection, constipation, pain, a new tablet such as an anticholinergic or levodopa, or a hospital stay.","Plan the order: what he has seen, any new cause, the diagnosis, the medicines, home and safety, then summary.","Have the leaflets ready: Lewy body dementia information, carers' assessment, falls service and the memory nurse's number."],"ask":["'Before I explain anything, what do you already know about his illness, and what were you hoping we could do today?'","'What exactly does he see, and does it frighten him or comfort him?'","'Are there days when he's nearly himself, and others when he's very muddled or drowsy?'","'What happens at night? Does he shout or act out his dreams?'","'Has anything changed recently: a water infection, constipation, pain, a new tablet?'","'How many times has he fallen this month? Has he hurt himself?'","'Is he still driving, and who sorts out his tablets?'","'How are you and the rest of the family coping?'"],"recognise":["Three core features of Lewy body dementia: attention and alertness that swing from day to day, detailed visual hallucinations of people or animals, and parkinsonism.","Memory came first, movement problems later, within the same year or so: that order points to Lewy body dementia rather than Parkinson's disease dementia.","A sudden change over days, more drowsiness or new confusion suggests delirium on top: check urine, bloods, bowels, pain and medicines.","Shouting or thrashing in sleep is REM sleep behaviour disorder, a supportive feature, not a night-time psychosis.","Low blood pressure on standing, slowness and stiffness all add to his falls; rivastigmine can slow the pulse and add to faints.","Hallucinations that comfort him and do not cause distress may need no medicine at all."],"decide":[{"c":"Explain the diagnosis honestly, increase rivastigmine to 4.5 mg twice daily after a pulse and ECG check, avoid antipsychotics, give non-drug strategies, and arrange falls, carers' and future planning support.","r":"Right: it uses the treatment most likely to help the visions, avoids the most dangerous one, and supports the family who carry the load.","best":true},{"c":"Start haloperidol 0.5 mg at night to stop the hallucinations and the night-time shouting.","r":"Dangerous: people with Lewy body dementia can have severe, sometimes fatal sensitivity reactions to antipsychotics, with rigidity, falls, drowsiness and confusion.","best":false},{"c":"Start levodopa to treat the stiffness and falls, as the scan shows a dopamine problem.","r":"Not first: levodopa does nothing for memory, helps movement less than in Parkinson's disease, and can worsen hallucinations. Consider later, at low dose, with the neurologist.","best":false}],"law":["Confidentiality: share only with Gerald's agreement, which is recorded. If he later loses capacity, act in his best interests and consult the family under the MCA 2005.","Lasting power of attorney: Gerald can make one for health and welfare and one for property and finance while he still has capacity for that decision; it only takes effect once the Office of the Public Guardian has registered it.","Driving: a dementia diagnosis must be reported to the DVLA. With fluctuating alertness and visual hallucinations he should not drive.","Carers' assessment: Ellis and his mother have a right to one from the local authority under the Care Act 2014."],"say":[{"to":"Grandson","s":"It sounds really hard to hear him chatting to your gran and his mum. Those visions are part of the illness. He isn't losing his mind, and he isn't doing it on purpose."},{"to":"Grandson","s":"I can't cure this, and I won't pretend otherwise. But there are good things we can do: raise his tablet slowly, make the house safer, and get you all more help."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"Dementia with Lewy bodies. Increasing rivastigmine to 4.5 mg BD; please check pulse and ECG before each step. Avoid antipsychotics and anticholinergics. Please screen for infection and constipation if he worsens suddenly."}],"write":"Memory clinic. Gerald Okafor, 82M. Seen with grandson Ellis (Gerald consented, letter on file).\nDx: dementia with Lewy bodies (DaT scan abnormal, memory 18/12, parkinsonism 9/12).\nEllis reports: well-formed visual hallucinations (late wife, his mother, cat, fox, children's faces), usually not distressing; up past midnight talking to them; good and bad days; shouting in sleep; 2 falls this month, no head injury; shouted at daughter one night thinking she was an intruder. No new infection on bloods 2/52 ago; bowels regular; no new medicines; not driving since last year.\nICE: hoped a tablet would stop the visions; read antipsychotics are dangerous; fears care home; mother exhausted.\nExplained: diagnosis, cause, DaT scan, why not PDD, progressive and not curable, rivastigmine, levodopa limits, antipsychotic sensitivity, non-drug strategies. Teach-back good.\nPlan: pulse 72 regular, ECG normal today; rivastigmine to 4.5 mg BD, review 2/52 for 6 mg BD. OT and physio referral; lying/standing BP; blister pack. Carers' assessment referral; LPA information; memory nurse as contact. Leaflets given. Review 4/52.\nSafety net: sudden confusion or drowsiness, GP same day or NHS 111; fall with head injury, 999.\nDr Chinonso, ST4","make":["Ask what he hopes for before explaining: he may want a cure, and the conversation has to meet that hope honestly.","Check for delirium every time behaviour worsens: a urine infection or constipation can mimic the illness getting worse.","Use his words: 'the cat on the sofa', 'talking to Gran'. Link each one to a feature of the illness.","Say the antipsychotic warning plainly and early; the family may meet a doctor who reaches for one at 3 a.m.","Pair every 'no' with a 'yes': no cure, but a higher dose; no haloperidol, but light, routine and reassurance.","Ask how the carers are doing: the family's exhaustion is often what breaks a home placement."],"next":"Gerald's pulse stays above 60 and rivastigmine reaches 6 mg twice daily after a month. The fox and the children's faces fade; he still sees his wife, which comforts him. The OT fits rails and a pendant alarm, and he falls once in two months. Ellis's mother has a carers' assessment and two respite nights a month, and Gerald signs both LPAs with the family.","marks":["Confirmed consent, explored Ellis's understanding, worries and hopes.","Clarified the hallucinations, fluctuations, night-time behaviour, falls and aggression.","Checked for infection, constipation, pain and new medicines before blaming the dementia.","Explained the diagnosis, the DaT scan, why it is not Parkinson's disease dementia, and the prognosis.","Explained rivastigmine, levodopa limits and the danger of antipsychotics.","Agreed home strategies, falls and carer support, future planning and a safety net."],"pitfalls":["Prescribing or suggesting haloperidol or another antipsychotic for the visions.","Promising that levodopa or rivastigmine will cure or halt the illness.","Missing delirium as the cause of a recent decline.","Advising the family to correct or argue with the hallucinations.","Ignoring the carers' exhaustion, driving and power of attorney."],"refs":["1. NICE NG97 Dementia (2018; checked Nov 2026)","2. McKeith IG et al. DLB Consortium criteria, 4th report. Neurology 2017","3. BNF: rivastigmine; antipsychotics in dementia (checked Nov 2026)","4. NHS website: Dementia with Lewy bodies (checked Nov 2026)","5. GOV.UK: Dementia and driving, DVLA (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, antipsychotics in dementia are tightly audited, and in Lewy body dementia even small doses can be lethal. Families often know this from charities' websites; agree with them out loud, and write 'avoid antipsychotics' in the GP letter.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-60","n":1360,"prog":"Core: first weeks and on call","icon":"🌪️","title":"Mania not settling on PICU: the nurse in charge wants lithium today","src":"","moment":{"when":"Sunday 11:30","where":"Psychiatric intensive care unit, nursing office","text":"Mr Connor Blackwood, a 26-year-old man with bipolar I disorder, was admitted to PICU three days ago in a manic episode. He is on olanzapine 10 mg at night, refuses bloods and observations, and keeps getting into other patients' faces. Yesterday one nearly hit him. Aisha, the nurse in charge, meets you at the door: 'Dr Hollis said lithium if the olanzapine didn't work. We want it started today.'"},"task":"Discuss treatment options with the nurse in charge: gather the presentation, treatment so far, physical health and risk, explain why lithium today is unsafe, and agree a practical plan for today that addresses her concerns.","first60":["Read the drug chart: olanzapine dose and timing, doses taken or refused, and PRN use in the last 24 hours.","Check his legal status and the consent to treatment position (within the first three months, or T2/T3 forms if relevant).","Look for any physical observations, fluid and food charts, and whether any bloods or ECG exist from previous admissions.","Read the incident reports: the near-assault yesterday and any others.","Plan to listen to Aisha's worry first; she is stretched and wants action, and she should leave with some."],"ask":["'What's worrying you most about Connor today?'","'How is he presenting: sleep, eating, drinking, speech, mood?'","'What has he actually taken, and how much PRN has he needed?'","'Has he let anyone take bloods or do observations?'","'What happened yesterday with the other patient? Is anyone else at risk?'","'What did Dr Hollis say exactly about lithium?'"],"recognise":["Three days on olanzapine 10 mg is too short and too low to call it a failure; 15 mg is the usual starting dose for mania alone.","Lithium needs baseline renal, thyroid and calcium tests and regular levels; without bloods it risks toxicity or useless levels.","He is at risk from others as well as a risk to them: intrusiveness provokes assault.","Refusing food and fluids in a manic, hyperactive man risks dehydration, which also makes lithium more dangerous.","Suspicion about blood tests may ease with a trusted nurse, a clear explanation and time.","The nurse's request is reasonable in spirit: she needs a plan that changes things today."],"decide":[{"c":"Decline lithium today with clear reasons, increase olanzapine, prescribe PRN lorazepam, raise observations, nurse him away from others, monitor food, fluids and physical health, keep trying bloods, and hand over to the consultant on Monday.","r":"Right: safe, evidence-based, and it gives the ward real tools for today.","best":true},{"c":"Start lithium 400 mg tonight because the consultant mentioned it.","r":"Unsafe: no baseline renal, thyroid or calcium tests, no way to check levels, and possible dehydration. Toxicity could be missed.","best":false},{"c":"Say no to lithium because it's the consultant's decision, and leave the plan as it is.","r":"Unhelpful: it ignores a real risk on the ward today and leaves the team without a plan.","best":false}],"law":["If detained, treatment for mental disorder in the first three months can be given without consent under section 63 and section 58; record capacity and consent discussions.","Rapid tranquillisation must follow local policy and NICE NG10: oral first, then IM if needed, with physical monitoring afterwards.","Safeguarding: other patients on PICU are adults at risk; record the near-assault and protect vulnerable patients.","Enhanced observations must be reviewed regularly and be the least restrictive option that keeps people safe."],"say":[{"to":"Nurse in charge","s":"I agree he needs more help today. Lithium isn't safe without blood tests, so here's what we can do right now."},{"to":"Nurse in charge","s":"He's only had three days of olanzapine at 10 mg, which is below the usual dose for mania. Let's go to 15 tonight."},{"to":"Consultant (handover)","s":"Olanzapine increased to 15 mg; PRN lorazepam; within-eyesight observations; bloods still refused. Please review lithium once baseline tests are possible."}],"write":"On-call review, PICU. Mr C Blackwood, 26M, bipolar I, current manic episode. Day 3 of admission. Detained.\nDiscussed with nurse in charge (Aisha). Presentation: elated, pressured speech, sleeping 2 to 3 h, eating little, drinking some fluids; suspicious that staff will experiment on his blood; refusing bloods and obs. Intrusive into others' space; near-assault by another patient yesterday. No self-harm.\nTreatment: olanzapine 10 mg nocte x3 (taken with persuasion); PRN lorazepam used x1.\nLithium request: declined today. Reasons: no baseline U&E, eGFR, TFTs, calcium, ECG; levels cannot be monitored; possible dehydration; olanzapine trial too short and below usual mania dose.\nPlan: olanzapine to 15 mg nocte from tonight; lorazepam 1 to 2 mg PO PRN, max 4 mg/24 h; obs increased to within eyesight; nurse away from vulnerable patients, low-stimulus area; food and fluid chart; visual obs (resp rate, colour, behaviour) if hands-on refused; named nurse to retry bloods; safeguarding record of incident. Call duty doctor if violence, fluid intake falls, or physical concern.\nHandover to Dr Hollis Monday re lithium.\nDr Chinonso ST4 (on call)","make":["Start with her worry and what she wants; say you agree he needs more help.","Gather before deciding: presentation, drug chart, PRN, bloods, obs, incidents.","Say 'not today' to lithium with specific reasons: bloods, monitoring, dose and duration, hydration.","Never stop at no; give a full plan for today: dose, PRN, observation, environment.","Cover physical health: food, fluids, visual observations, when to call you.","Handover to the consultant and document clearly."],"next":"Connor takes olanzapine 15 mg that night and sleeps five hours. On within-eyesight observations in the quieter lounge there are no further incidents. On Tuesday, after his named nurse explains each test, he allows bloods and an ECG. Dr Hollis reviews him and plans to add lithium if he is not settling on the higher dose.","marks":["Explored her concerns and expectations, including the consultant's comment about lithium.","Gathered the presentation, treatment so far, adherence and PRN use.","Checked physical health: bloods, observations, food and fluids.","Assessed risk to and from other patients.","Explained why lithium today is unsafe and why olanzapine has not had a fair trial.","Agreed a concrete plan: dose increase, PRN, observations, environment, handover."],"pitfalls":["Prescribing lithium to keep the peace.","Saying no only because it's the consultant's decision.","Giving no alternative plan.","Forgetting dehydration and physical monitoring.","Ignoring the risk to Connor from other patients."],"refs":["1. NICE CG185 Bipolar disorder: assessment and management (2014, updated 2023; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG10: short-term management of violence and aggression (2015; checked Nov 2026)","3. BNF: olanzapine, lithium, lorazepam (checked Nov 2026)","4. MHA Code of Practice, ch.s 24 and 26 (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK, nurses are expected to challenge doctors and doctors to explain their reasoning. On call, a respectful 'not that, but this' with clear reasons is exactly what's wanted. Leave every decision in writing for the Monday team.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-61","n":1361,"prog":"Old Age Psychiatry","icon":"🏡","title":"Low mood in an older carer: symptoms, suicide risk and risk to his wife","src":"","moment":{"when":"Tuesday 14:00, home visit","where":"Older adults community mental health team, the patient's home","text":"Desmond Clarke, an 83-year-old retired bus driver, has cared for his wife Margaret at home since her vascular dementia was diagnosed last year. His GP has referred him because his mood keeps sinking; routine bloods are normal. He has osteoarthritis in his knees and hands. Margaret is watching television in the next room when you arrive."},"task":"Take a history of his current symptoms, including the strain of caring, physical health and substances; screen for psychotic features; and carry out a thorough assessment of suicide risk and of risk to Margaret.","first60":["Read the GP letter: bloods, current prescriptions (especially painkillers and anything sedating), and any mention of drink or falls.","Check whether anyone has told adult social care about Margaret, and whether a carers' assessment has ever been done.","On arrival, look around: food in the kitchen, medicine boxes on the side, how he walks, how Margaret looks.","Ask for a private room within earshot of Margaret, so he can talk freely and still feel she is safe.","Plan the order: mood, the caring, his body, then the hard risk questions, including about Margaret, before you summarise."],"ask":["'How's your mood been over the last few weeks, compared with a year ago?'","'What do you still enjoy? What have you stopped doing?'","'What time do you wake, and what goes through your mind when you do?'","'What does a normal day and night of looking after Margaret involve? Who helps you?'","'Do you ever feel you have done something wrong, or deserve to be punished? Do you ever feel your body or money is failing in a way others can't see?'","'How much do you drink over a week? Which painkillers do you take, and are any tablets saved up in the house?'","'Have you had thoughts about ending your life? Have you thought about how?'","'Have you ever thought it would be better if you and Margaret both died? Have you ever felt like hurting her?'"],"recognise":["Early waking, weight loss, loss of interest and guilt over months point to a depressive episode, not just tiredness from caring.","Late-life depression often shows as physical complaints, poor concentration and irritability more than sadness; ask directly.","Guilt that is fixed and out of proportion, or beliefs of ruin, illness or being already dead, mean psychotic depression.","Memory complaints in a low older man may be depression; note them and plan a cognitive screen once mood lifts.","Old man, isolated, chronic pain, carer, hopeless, drinking more, tablets in the house: several risk factors stacked together.","'We'd both be better off gone' is a joint death thought: it raises risk to Margaret as well as to him."],"decide":[{"c":"Complete the history and risk assessment, explain that this looks like depression, agree removal of saved tablets, phone his daughter with his consent, discuss with the consultant today for urgent follow-up, and start a carers' assessment and support for Margaret.","r":"Right: it matches the level of risk, treats both people in the household, and gives him a reason to accept help.","best":true},{"c":"Tell him caring is exhausting, advise him to see the GP about sleeping tablets, and discharge back to primary care.","r":"Wrong: misses a depressive episode with suicidal and joint-death thoughts, and hypnotics add falls and overdose risk.","best":false},{"c":"Arrange immediate admission for both him and Margaret today.","r":"Disproportionate right now: he has no plan, strong protective factors and accepts help. Escalate to home treatment or admission if intent appears or the plan fails.","best":false}],"law":["Care Act 2014: the local authority must offer a carer's assessment to any carer who appears to need support, and assess Margaret's own care needs.","Safeguarding: if you believe Margaret is at risk of neglect or harm, discuss with your senior and raise a safeguarding concern with adult social care.","Confidentiality: ask his consent to involve his daughter; if you judge there is a serious risk to life, you may share without consent (GMC).","He has capacity and is informal; admission is voluntary unless risk rises to a level that needs Mental Health Act assessment."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"You've been doing a full-time job, day and night, with no break. Feeling this low isn't a weakness; it sounds like depression, and it can be treated."},{"to":"Patient","s":"Some people caring for someone they love start to think it would be easier if they both went. Has anything like that crossed your mind?"},{"to":"Consultant (phone)","s":"83-year-old male carer, moderate to severe depressive episode, no psychotic features. Passive death wishes including 'both better off gone', no plan, saved co-codamol at home now handed over. Daughter aware. I'd like urgent follow-up this week and a carers' assessment."}],"write":"Home visit, older adults CMHT. 83M, retired bus driver. Main carer for wife Margaret (vascular dementia, 1 yr). Referred by GP: low mood. Bloods normal.\nHx: mood low ~1 yr, much worse 3 months. Wakes 4 am, ~4 h sleep. Anhedonia (given up allotment). Low energy, poor concentration. Appetite down, ~half a stone lost. Guilt: 'failing Margaret'; hopeless. No diurnal variation reported.\nCaring: Margaret wanders at night, needs help washing and dressing. No paid carers, no respite. Daughter Karen 1 hr away. Frustration; shouted once at night, no physical harm.\nPhysical: OA knees and hands, pain poorly controlled; ibuprofen OTC and co-codamol. One fall in garden 2 months ago. Hearing aid. Reports forgetfulness.\nNo nihilistic, guilty or poverty delusions; no hallucinations.\nAlcohol: 2-3 whiskies nightly (~25 units/wk), increased. Tin of saved co-codamol (~30 tabs).\nRisk: passive death wishes; 'we'd both be better off gone'; no plan or preparations; thought of the tin. Protective: grandchildren, daughter, duty to Margaret. No intent to harm Margaret.\nImpression: moderate to severe depressive episode, no psychotic features. Risk to self significant; risk to Margaret (neglect, joint-death thoughts) present.\nPlan: tablets handed to me for disposal; daughter called with consent; discussed with consultant; urgent review in 48 h; carers' assessment and needs assessment for Margaret requested; crisis numbers given.\nDr Chinonso, CT2","make":["Make privacy practical: he will not talk about Margaret if he thinks she can hear, and he will not leave her alone.","Ask about the nights: night-time wandering is often the thing that breaks a carer.","Count the biological symptoms with numbers: hours slept, weight lost, time he wakes.","Ask about the tablets in the house in every older person who is low; check what is saved, not just prescribed.","Ask the joint-death question plainly and calmly; he will not raise it himself.","Name both people as needing help: a plan for him alone leaves the main risk in place."],"next":"His daughter comes the next day and takes the saved tablets. The CMHT nurse visits twice in the first week, the consultant starts sertraline, and social care arranges two night sits a week and a day centre place for Margaret. Six weeks later he is sleeping to 6 am and back at the allotment on Saturdays.","marks":["Arranged privacy while keeping Margaret safe, and explained the purpose of the visit.","Elicited core and biological depressive symptoms with duration and change over time.","Explored the caring role: tasks, nights, help available, burden and guilt.","Covered pain, mobility, falls, senses and memory, and asked about alcohol and medicines.","Screened for psychotic features of depression.","Assessed suicide risk in depth, including means at home, and asked directly about risk to Margaret."],"pitfalls":["Treating his low mood as 'understandable' and missing a depressive episode.","Forgetting to ask about saved or prescribed tablets and alcohol.","Never asking whether he has thought about both of them dying.","Leaving out Margaret's safety and the need for support for her.","Offering sleeping tablets as the answer."],"refs":["1. NICE NG222 (adult depression, 2022), treatment and management; checked Nov 2026","2. NICE NG225 Self-harm: assessment, management and preventing recurrence (2022; checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE NG150 Supporting adult carers (2020; checked Nov 2026)","4. Care Act 2014: carers' assessments (checked Nov 2026)","5. NHS website: Depression in older people; Carers' assessment (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK many older couples live alone with no family nearby, and carers often hide how bad things are. Social care, not health, funds most home help, so a carer's assessment is often the most powerful thing you arrange.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-68","n":1368,"prog":"Old Age Psychiatry","icon":"🚗","title":"Mum's memory, whisky and driving: explaining results and stopping an unsafe driver","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 15:30, memory clinic","where":"Memory clinic, older adults' community mental health team (phone call)","text":"Mrs Margaret Doyle, an 81-year-old widow, scored 50/100 on the ACE-III, losing most points on memory and visuospatial tasks. She looked unkempt and smelled of stale alcohol. Her GP has since found she drinks half a bottle of whisky or more a day, with raised ALT and GGT. Twice told not to drive, she refused. Her son Stephen, 52, rings: 'She's livid. Says you're taking her car.' She has agreed you can talk to him."},"task":"Confirm consent, hear the son's concerns, explain the test, the alcohol findings and what they may mean, then agree immediate and longer-term action on her driving.","first60":["Check the record: her written consent to share with Stephen, the ACE-III breakdown, GP letter and blood results.","Confirm you are speaking to Stephen and that his mother agreed; do not go further until you have.","Hold the order: his story, the test, the drinking, what it may mean, driving now, longer term.","Know the GMC sequence for driving before you start: her duty, one more try, then the doctor tells the DVLA.","Expect anger on her behalf and guilt on his; name both."],"ask":["'What have you been told, and what's worrying you most?'","'How has she been managing at home: bills, meals, washing?'","'How has her driving been? Where does she drive?'","'What have you noticed about her drinking?'","'How do you think she'll take the news about driving?'","'What does driving mean to her, and what would replace it?'"],"recognise":["ACE-III 50/100 is far below the usual cut-offs of 88 and 82, with memory and visuospatial losses: a pattern that suggests dementia of moderate severity.","Half a 70 cl bottle of 40% whisky is about 14 units, a week's low-risk limit in one day.","Raised GGT reflects heavy drinking; raised ALT reflects liver cell injury.","Heavy drinking can lower the score, so the cause may be dementia, alcohol-related brain damage, or both; a repeat test after abstinence helps separate them.","Scrapes, getting lost and refusal of advice, with poor visuospatial skills and alcohol, mean she is not safe to drive now.","Stopping heavy drinking abruptly at her age risks withdrawal fits and delirium."],"decide":[{"c":"Tell Stephen plainly she must stop driving now; ask her once more to stop and tell the DVLA herself; if she won't, tell her the clinic will inform the DVLA, then do so and write to her. Start thiamine, plan supervised alcohol reduction and further tests.","r":"Right: follows the GMC sequence, protects the public and her, and treats the alcohol safely.","best":true},{"c":"Suggest a specialist driving assessment so she can keep driving meanwhile.","r":"Wrong: her deficits are too great to keep driving while waiting, and offering this as a way out sends a mixed message.","best":false},{"c":"Ask Stephen to take her keys today without telling her why.","r":"Not first: keys or disabling the car are last resorts for immediate danger. Start with honest explanation, her legal duty and the DVLA route.","best":false}],"law":["She has consented to sharing with Stephen; keep to what she agreed and note it.","Drivers must tell the DVLA about dementia and alcohol misuse. GMC guidance: if a patient continues to drive when unfit, try to persuade her, tell her you will inform the DVLA, do so promptly, then write to her.","If she lacks the understanding to follow the advice, the DVLA is told straight away.","Driving after the licence is revoked is an offence; police involvement is a last resort.","Lasting Power of Attorney can only be made while she has capacity to make it."],"say":[{"to":"Son","s":"I'm sorry, Stephen. From what we know, your mum isn't safe to drive, and she needs to stop now, not after more tests."},{"to":"Son","s":"The drink could be part of the memory problem, but a score of fifty is still very low. We need to treat the drinking safely and test her again."},{"to":"GP (letter)","s":"ACE-III 50/100 (memory, visuospatial). Whisky ~14 units/day, raised ALT/GGT. Advised to stop driving; DVLA to be informed if she does not. Please start thiamine 100 mg tds and refer to the community alcohol team for supervised reduction; MRI head requested."}],"write":"Memory clinic. Phone call with son, Stephen Doyle (52), re Mrs Margaret Doyle, 81F. Her verbal consent to share recorded by nurse 3/11.\nSon: months of decline, missed bills, poor self-care; scraped car twice this spring, lost driving to his house once; unaware of drinking until neighbour noticed bottles. Angry and worried.\nExplained: ACE-III 50/100 (memory, visuospatial); unkempt, smelled of alcohol; GP: >= half bottle whisky daily (~14 units/day), raised ALT and GGT. Possible dementia, alcohol-related brain damage or both; MRI, bloods, repeat ACE-III after abstinence.\nDriving: unsafe now. Advised she must stop and inform DVLA. Plan: joint meeting with consultant and son this week; if she refuses or keeps driving, clinic informs DVLA (tell her first, write after). Police/keys only if immediate danger.\nAlcohol: not to stop suddenly; thiamine 100 mg tds via GP; community alcohol team for supervised reduction.\nCarers' assessment offered; LPA discussed; adult social care referral with her consent.\nTeach-back good. Dr Chinonso, ST5","make":["Confirm consent before giving any result.","Ask what he has seen before explaining: the scrapes and getting lost come only if asked.","Give the score, the cut-offs and where she lost points, in plain words.","Turn ALT and GGT into 'liver strain' and 'heavy drinking', and say never to stop suddenly.","Say 'she isn't safe to drive' plainly, then give the reasons.","Follow the order: her duty, one more try, then the DVLA, then last resorts."],"next":"At a joint meeting with the consultant and Stephen, Mrs Doyle shouts, then agrees to surrender her licence; Stephen sells the car and sets up a taxi account. With thiamine and a supervised reduction she cuts to two units a day. Her MRI shows atrophy with small vessel change, and her ACE-III three months later is 58.","marks":["Confirmed consent and drew out his concerns and observations.","Explained the ACE-III score, cut-offs and pattern.","Explained the drinking, the liver tests and why not to stop suddenly.","Explained the possible causes and the further tests.","Stated clearly she must stop driving and set out the DVLA sequence.","Agreed practical and longer-term steps and supported Stephen."],"pitfalls":["Giving results before confirming consent.","Blaming it all on the whisky and promising the memory will recover.","Offering a driving test as a way to keep driving now.","Leading with the police or taking the keys.","Advising her to stop drinking at once."],"refs":["1. DVLA: Assessing fitness to drive, a guide for medical professionals (checked Nov 2026)","2. GMC: Confidentiality, patients' fitness to drive and reporting concerns to the DVLA (checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE NG97 Dementia: assessment, management and support (2018; checked Nov 2026)","4. NICE CG100 Alcohol-use disorders: physical complications (2010, updated 2017; checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in the UK the driver, not the doctor, holds the legal duty to tell the DVLA, but the GMC expects doctors to inform the DVLA if a patient keeps driving against advice. Families often expect the doctor to 'take the licence'; explain that only the DVLA can.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-69","n":1369,"prog":"Core: first weeks and on call","icon":"🖐️","title":"Numb, tingling right arm: upper limb sensory and motor examination on admission","src":"","moment":{"when":"Monday 12:15, admission day","where":"Clinic room, adult acute ward","text":"Tomasz Nowak, a 45-year-old man, came in informally this morning with depression and somatisation disorder. For four weeks he has had numbness and pins and needles in his right arm. The ward team want to be sure there is no neurological cause before the ward round. The history is already in the notes. The nurse asks you to examine both arms now."},"task":"Without taking a history, examine the sensory and motor systems of both upper limbs, comparing sides, and report whether there is any neurological deficit and what happens next.","first60":["Read the admission note: which part of the arm, any neck pain, diabetes, alcohol, B12 or thyroid history; do not ask it all again.","Gather kit: tendon hammer, 128 Hz tuning fork, cotton wool, a neurotip, and cold metal for temperature.","Offer a chaperone, clean your hands, and ask him to undress to the waist or a vest so both arms and shoulders are visible.","Plan the order: inspect, tone, power, reflexes, coordination, sensation; always both sides, right against left.","Treat the symptom as real; a somatisation label is never a reason to skip the examination."],"ask":["'Is it alright if I examine both arms? It takes about ten minutes.'","'Would you like a chaperone?'","'Is there any pain anywhere before I move your arms?'","'Push up against me... now pull me towards you.'","'Eyes closed now; tell me yes whenever you feel a touch.'","'Does this feel the same on both sides?'"],"recognise":["Wasting, fasciculation, reduced tone, weakness and absent reflexes suggest a lower motor neuron problem: root, plexus, nerve or anterior horn.","Increased tone, pronator drift, brisk reflexes and a positive Hoffmann's sign suggest an upper motor neuron problem.","Sensory loss in a dermatome points to a root (for example C6 thumb); in the lateral three and a half fingers it points to the median nerve.","The dorsal columns carry light touch, vibration and joint position; the spinothalamic tracts carry pain and temperature. Test both.","A normal, symmetrical examination makes a structural cause unlikely but does not exclude carpal tunnel, early neuropathy or B12 deficiency."],"decide":[{"c":"Complete a full, comparative examination; record that it is normal; arrange bloods (B12, folate, HbA1c, TFT, FBC, U&E) and re-examine if symptoms change.","r":"Right: rules out a deficit properly, screens treatable causes, and takes the symptom seriously.","best":true},{"c":"Skip the examination because his symptoms are part of somatisation disorder.","r":"Unsafe: people with somatisation also get physical illness, and missing a cord or nerve lesion causes harm.","best":false},{"c":"Request an urgent MRI of the cervical spine before examining.","r":"Premature: imaging follows clinical signs. Examine first; red flags or new signs then guide imaging.","best":false}],"law":["Informal patient: valid consent is needed for each part of the examination; he may stop at any time.","Offer a chaperone and record the offer and the reply (GMC intimate and close examinations guidance applies to undressing).","Record findings the same day so the ward round can act on them."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Your symptoms are real, and I want to check the nerves and muscles properly. I'll compare both arms, so you'll do everything twice."},{"to":"Patient","s":"The good news is that strength, reflexes and feeling are the same on both sides. That's reassuring, and we'll still check some bloods for causes of tingling."},{"to":"Ward round","s":"Upper limb neurological check in Mr Nowak: no wasting or fasciculation, tone, power, reflexes and coordination normal and symmetrical, sensation intact in all modalities C5-T1. No deficit. Bloods sent."}],"write":"Upper limb neurological examination. Tomasz Nowak, 45M, informal, admitted today (depression, somatisation disorder). 4/52 numbness and paraesthesia R arm (history in admission note).\nConsent given; chaperone offered and declined. Undressed to vest.\nInspection: normal posture, no wasting, fasciculation, tremor, scars or skin changes. Normal nails and joints. Radial pulse 76 regular.\nTone: normal at wrist and elbow bilaterally.\nPower: MRC 5/5 bilaterally: shoulder abduction, elbow flexion/extension, wrist extension/flexion, finger extension/flexion/abduction, thumb abduction.\nReflexes: biceps, triceps, supinator 2+ and symmetrical; Hoffmann's negative bilaterally.\nCoordination: finger-nose normal, no dysdiadochokinesis, no pronator drift.\nSensation: light touch, pin prick, temperature, vibration (128 Hz) and joint position intact and symmetrical, C5-T1. Tinel's and Phalen's negative.\nImpression: no objective neurological deficit.\nPlan: B12, folate, HbA1c, TFT, FBC, U&E, calcium. Re-examine if new symptoms. Explained to patient. Dr Chinonso, CT1","make":["Explain why you are examining, so he does not hear 'it's all in your head'.","Compare every test right with left, and say so.","Test power joint by joint and name the root you are testing in your head.","Reinforce reflexes before calling them absent.","Test both sensory pathways: dorsal columns and spinothalamic.","Close with a clear result and a next step, not just 'normal'."],"next":"The examination and bloods are normal. With his consent the team explains that the tingling is real and often linked to anxiety and tension, and his CBT includes work on bodily symptoms. Two weeks later, he reports the tingling is less frequent.","marks":["Gained consent, offered a chaperone, exposed both arms and asked about pain.","Inspected for wasting, fasciculation, scars and tremor.","Tested tone and power by joint, comparing sides with MRC grades.","Tested biceps, triceps and supinator reflexes, with reinforcement, and Hoffmann's.","Tested coordination and pronator drift.","Tested all sensory modalities by dermatome and summarised the findings and next steps."],"pitfalls":["Retaking his story when the history is already in the notes.","Examining only the right arm.","Leaving out pain and temperature sensation.","Calling a reflex absent without reinforcement.","Dismissing the symptom as somatic because the examination is normal."],"refs":["1. GMC: Intimate examinations and chaperones (checked Nov 2026)","2. Medical Research Council: Aids to the examination of the peripheral nervous system (MRC power scale; checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE CKS: Carpal tunnel syndrome (checked Nov 2026)","4. NICE CKS: Neck pain, cervical radiculopathy (checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: UK wards expect psychiatric trainees to do and document full physical examinations on admission. Always offer and record a chaperone. Never let a psychiatric label stop you examining: diagnostic overshadowing is a recognised cause of harm.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"},
{"id":"c-t-70","n":1370,"prog":"Core: first weeks and on call","icon":"🫀","title":"Heart and vessels in memory decline: a cardiovascular examination with a student","src":"","moment":{"when":"Wednesday 10:30, ward physical health review","where":"Examination room, older adults' acute ward","text":"Desmond Clarke, a 57-year-old man, was admitted with worsening memory and planning problems. He has had type 2 diabetes for five years and has fallen several times recently. The consultant wonders about vascular dementia. A third-year medical student, Priya, is shadowing you and asks what you are looking for. Des says, 'Call me Des.'"},"task":"Carry out a full cardiovascular examination looking for signs that support vascular dementia and explain the falls, talking the student through what each step looks for and why.","first60":["Check the drug chart for antihypertensives, sulfonylureas or insulin, and a recent capillary glucose: low sugar and postural drop both cause falls.","Gather kit: stethoscope, manual sphygmomanometer, a couch that tilts to 45 degrees, a tape for pulse timing.","Keep instructions short and one at a time; his memory is the reason he is here.","Plan to speak to Des first, then to Priya, so he never feels talked over.","Hold the order: general, hands and pulse, BP, neck, heart, lungs and legs, then completion."],"ask":["'Is it alright if I examine your heart and blood vessels? Would you like a chaperone?'","'Any chest pain, or pain anywhere I should avoid?'","'Could you lie back at an angle and take your top off?'","'I'll take your blood pressure lying, then after a minute standing. Tell me if you feel dizzy.'","'Hold your breath for me for a few seconds.'"],"recognise":["Atrial fibrillation (irregularly irregular pulse) often sends clots to the brain, causing strokes and multi-infarct dementia.","Hypertension, diabetes, smoking and raised cholesterol are the core risk factors for small vessel disease and vascular dementia.","Carotid bruits suggest carotid stenosis and a source of emboli; their absence does not exclude it.","A systolic fall of 20 mmHg or more on standing suggests postural hypotension, a common, treatable cause of falls in diabetes.","Aortic stenosis (slow-rising pulse, ejection systolic murmur to the carotids) causes exertional dizziness and falls.","Xanthelasma, corneal arcus before 60, weak foot pulses and nicotine staining point to vascular disease elsewhere."],"decide":[{"c":"Complete the examination, record lying and standing BP, then arrange ECG, lipids, HbA1c, U&E, TFT, B12, urine ACR, and MRI brain; review drugs that drop BP or sugar, and refer findings to the medical team.","r":"Right: links the signs to his memory and falls and turns them into treatable steps.","best":true},{"c":"Examine the heart sounds only, since the question is dementia.","r":"Incomplete: pulse rhythm, BP lying and standing, carotids and foot pulses are where the vascular evidence lies.","best":false},{"c":"Do a silent examination and explain everything at the end.","r":"Misses the teaching task, and Des is left unsure what is happening at each step.","best":false}],"law":["Assume capacity unless shown otherwise; consent is needed for each part, and he can stop at any time.","Ask Des before a student watches or takes part; he may say no without affecting his care.","Offer and record a chaperone, especially when exposing the chest."],"say":[{"to":"Patient","s":"Your heart and blood vessels can affect memory and balance, so I'd like to check them carefully. Tell me if anything is uncomfortable."},{"to":"Student","s":"I'm feeling the pulse for rhythm first. An irregularly irregular pulse means atrial fibrillation, which can throw clots to the brain."},{"to":"Ward round","s":"CVS examination: regular pulse 82, BP 162/96 lying, 138/88 standing at 1 min with dizziness, xanthelasma, weak left foot pulses, no bruits or murmurs. Supports vascular risk; postural drop may explain falls."}],"write":"Cardiovascular examination. Desmond Clarke, 57M. Cognitive decline, T2DM 5 yrs, recent falls. Consent given; chaperone (HCA) present; student observing with consent.\nGeneral: comfortable at rest, not breathless. Bilateral xanthelasma; no corneal arcus. Clothes a little unkempt. Insulin sites: none (oral agents only). Capillary glucose 6.8.\nHands: nicotine staining R index and middle fingers. Radial pulse 82, regular, normal volume; no radio-radial delay.\nBP: R 162/96, L 158/94 (lying); R 138/88 standing at 1 min, felt dizzy: postural drop 24 mmHg.\nNeck: JVP not raised. Carotid pulses normal; no bruits.\nPraecordium: apex 5th ICS MCL, undisplaced; no heaves or thrills. HS I+II+0, no murmurs.\nChest: clear bases. Legs: no oedema. Femoral pulses present; R foot pulses present, L DP absent and PT weak. Feet: dry skin, no ulcers; reduced monofilament sensation both soles.\nImpression: hypertension, peripheral arterial disease, smoker, postural hypotension. Supports vascular contribution to cognitive decline; postural drop and neuropathy may explain falls.\nPlan: ECG, lipids, HbA1c, U&E, eGFR, FBC, TFT, B12, folate, urine ACR; MRI brain; drug review for BP-lowering and hypoglycaemic agents; ABPI and diabetic foot review; medical team and GP informed; smoking cessation offer. Dr Chinonso, CT2","make":["Talk to Des first, then turn to the student; he should never feel like a specimen.","Feel the pulse for a full 30 seconds before deciding it is regular.","Measure BP in both arms, then lying and standing; this is where his falls may be explained.","Listen to the carotids with breath held before listening to the heart.","Finish at the feet: pulses, skin and sensation in a man with diabetes.","Close by linking each finding to his memory and falls, and say what you would do next."],"next":"The ECG shows sinus rhythm with left ventricular hypertrophy. MRI shows extensive white matter change and two old lacunar infarcts. Doxazosin, started by his GP, is stopped and his postural drop resolves. He starts atorvastatin and a smoking cessation plan, and his falls stop.","marks":["Gained consent for himself and the student, offered a chaperone, positioned at 45 degrees.","Looked for general vascular signs: xanthelasma, arcus, breathlessness, self-neglect.","Assessed pulse rate, rhythm and radio-radial delay, naming AF.","Measured BP in both arms and lying and standing.","Examined JVP, carotid pulses and carotid bruits.","Examined the praecordium, lung bases, ankles and foot pulses, and explained each step's purpose."],"pitfalls":["A silent examination when explanation is part of the task.","Skipping lying and standing BP in a man who falls.","Missing carotid auscultation.","Forgetting the feet in a man with diabetes.","Talking only to the student and losing Des."],"refs":["1. NICE NG136 Hypertension in adults: diagnosis and management (2019, updated 2023; checked Nov 2026)","2. NICE NG97 Dementia: assessment, management and support (2018; checked Nov 2026)","3. NICE NG196 Atrial fibrillation: diagnosis and management (2021; checked Nov 2026)","4. NICE NG19 Diabetic foot problems (2015, updated 2019; checked Nov 2026)"],"img":"Tip for IMGs: in UK ward teaching, patients are asked before students watch or help, and their answer is respected. Psychiatry trainees are expected to do and record a full physical examination; vascular findings often change the dementia diagnosis and the treatment.","checked":"7 Nov 2026"}
);
